// 1. Core Study Guide (guide-data.js — 15 chapters)

window.MECHPREP_GUIDE = [
{id:'math',name:'Engineering Mathematics',group:'Foundation',visual:'math',syllabus:'Linear algebra · Calculus · Differential equations · Probability and statistics · Numerical methods · Dimensional analysis',idea:'Turn a physical situation into a small set of variables, equations and boundary conditions. Check dimensions before doing long algebra.',formula:'Taylor: f(x+h) ≈ f(x)+h f′(x)+h²f″(x)/2 · Newton: xₙ₊₁=xₙ−f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ) · Mean: μ=Σxᵢ/n · σ²=Σ(xᵢ−μ)²/n',derive:'For Newton iteration, approximate f near xₙ by its tangent: 0 ≈ f(xₙ)+f′(xₙ)(xₙ₊₁−xₙ). Rearranging gives the update. It converges quickly near a simple root, but can fail when f′ is small or the initial guess is poor.',apply:'Solve characteristic roots, optimize design variables, estimate measurement uncertainty and fit experimental data. For a first-order system y′+ay=b, write the homogeneous solution plus a particular solution, then apply the initial condition.',trick:'Use units as an error detector; nondimensionalize before comparing scales. For a 2×2 matrix, trace and determinant give the characteristic polynomial λ²−tr(A)λ+det(A).',pitfall:'Do not assume Newton always converges. Distinguish population variance from sample variance; the latter uses n−1. State the independent-variable interval and boundary/initial conditions.',visual:'A curve is locally linear near a point; the tangent predicts the next Newton iterate.'},
{id:'aptitude',name:'General Aptitude',group:'Foundation',visual:'aptitude',syllabus:'Quantitative aptitude · Percentages and ratios · Averages · Work and time · Speed and distance · Data interpretation · Permutations and probability · Logical reasoning · Verbal ability',idea:'Translate words to relationships before calculating. Define the base quantity, preserve units, and estimate the answer range.',formula:'Work = rate × time · Distance = speed × time · Successive % change = (1+a)(1+b)−1 · P(A∪B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A∩B)',derive:'If a value changes by a then by b, start with 1 unit: after the first change it is (1+a); after the second it is (1+a)(1+b). The net change is ab+a+b, so successive percentages are not generally added.',apply:'For a work problem, take total work as an LCM of individual days, convert each person to units/day, add/subtract rates, then divide remaining work by the combined rate. For data interpretation, compute only the ratios needed and round consistently.',trick:'Assume a convenient total (often 100) for percentage mixes. Use ratio parts as multipliers. Check options with bounds before exact arithmetic. For relative speed, add speeds only when motion is toward each other.',pitfall:'Do not compare percentages with different bases. Do not average rates directly; average total distance over total time. In syllogisms, use only what is logically guaranteed.',visual:'Rates add when agents work together; inverse time follows from a fixed amount of work.'},
{id:'mechanics',name:'Engineering Mechanics',group:'Core Mechanical',visual:'mechanics',syllabus:'Force systems · Free-body diagrams · Equilibrium · Trusses · Friction · Centroids and area moments · Kinematics · Newton laws · Work-energy · Impulse-momentum',idea:'A free-body diagram isolates one body and shows every external force and couple. Choose axes that make the unknown reactions easy to resolve.',formula:'ΣF=ma · ΣMₒ=Iₒα · Work: W₁→₂=Δ(½mv²) · Impulse: ∫Fdt=Δ(mv) · Dry friction: |F|≤μN',derive:'For static equilibrium, acceleration is zero, so Newton’s second law becomes ΣF=0 and ΣM=0. For a planar rigid body, use two force equations and one moment equation. Take moments about a point that eliminates the most unknown reactions.',apply:'For a simply supported member, solve support reactions first; then cut the member and draw internal N, V and M. In dynamics, choose energy when forces are conservative and time is not asked; use impulse-momentum for short impacts.',trick:'A two-force member has equal, opposite, collinear end forces. A three-force member in equilibrium has concurrent lines of action (or is parallel). Take moments before resolving every force.',pitfall:'A pin reaction has two components; a roller on a smooth surface has one normal reaction. Friction opposes impending relative motion, not necessarily the visible motion. Draw forces on the body, not on its supports.',visual:'A beam rests on a pin and roller; load is balanced by support reactions.'},
{id:'som',name:'Strength of Materials',group:'Core Mechanical',visual:'som',syllabus:'Stress and strain · Axial loading · Thermal stress · Torsion · Shear-force and bending-moment diagrams · Beam stresses and deflection · Columns · Strain energy · Mohr circle · Failure theories',idea:'Start from equilibrium to obtain internal resultants; use geometry to convert resultants into stress; use the material law and boundary conditions to find deformation.',formula:'σ=F/A · ε=δ/L · τ=Tr/J · σᵦ=My/I · δₘₐₓ(center load, simple supports)=PL³/(48EI) · Euler Pcr=π²EI/(KL)²',derive:'For elastic axial loading, σ=Eε. With uniform area A and length L: δ=FL/(AE). For bending, a plane section remains plane; strain varies linearly with distance y, giving σ=My/I after integrating the stress distribution to match moment M.',apply:'Check max tension/compression, shear and deflection separately. Use a stress concentration factor at shoulders/holes. For a column, compare slenderness and end support conditions before using Euler buckling.',trick:'For a rectangular section I=bh³/12: doubling depth multiplies I by 8, while doubling width only doubles it. A quick sanity check: δ scales with L³, so a small span error has a large effect.',pitfall:'Use consistent units (N·mm with mm⁴, or N·m with m⁴). Do not use σ=My/I at a discontinuity without a concentration check. Euler applies to long, elastic columns with small initial crookedness.',visual:'A point load bends a simply supported beam; slider changes the load and reaction arrows.'},
{id:'materials',name:'Engineering Materials & Metallurgy',group:'Core Mechanical',visual:'materials',syllabus:'Crystal structure · Defects and diffusion · Phase diagrams · Fe–C diagram · Heat treatment · TTT/CCT · Hardenability · Fatigue · Creep · Fracture · Corrosion · Polymers and composites',idea:'Processing creates a microstructure; microstructure controls properties; properties and service conditions control failure. Identify the loading, temperature, environment and time scale.',formula:'Rule of mixtures (longitudinal): E_c≈V_fE_f+V_mE_m · Fick I: J=−D dc/dx · Fatigue life depends on stress amplitude, mean stress and surface/size factors',derive:'Diffusion follows the concentration gradient. Fick’s first law says flux is proportional to the negative gradient: atoms flow from high concentration to low concentration. For transient diffusion, Fick’s second law tracks concentration changing with time.',apply:'Choose quench/temper to trade hardness against toughness; normalize to refine grains; use precipitation hardening for suitable alloys. Diagnose fatigue by crack origin, beach marks and cyclic loading history.',trick:'Hardness often correlates with strength within a material family, but it is not a universal conversion. Compare fracture toughness K_IC with applied stress intensity before calling a crack safe.',pitfall:'Do not confuse hardenability (depth of hardening) with hardness (resistance to indentation). A phase diagram gives equilibrium phases, not transformation time; use TTT/CCT for cooling paths.',visual:'A temperature path crosses phase regions and changes the resulting microstructure.'},
{id:'tom',name:'Theory of Machines & Vibrations',group:'Core Mechanical',visual:'tom',syllabus:'Links and mechanisms · Velocity and acceleration · Gears and gear trains · Cams · Flywheels · Governors · Balancing · Free and forced vibration · Damping · Resonance',idea:'Use geometry for position, differentiate for velocity and acceleration, then apply force/moment balance for dynamic loads.',formula:'Gear pair: N₁T₁=N₂T₂ · ωₙ=√(k/m) · r=ω/ωₙ · X/F₀/k=1/√[(1−r²)²+(2ζr)²]',derive:'A single-degree-of-freedom oscillator obeys mẍ+cẋ+kx=F₀sinωt. Dividing by m defines natural frequency ωₙ=√(k/m), damping ratio ζ=c/(2√km) and frequency ratio r=ω/ωₙ; the steady-state amplitude follows from the sinusoidal response.',apply:'For gears, calculate direction separately from ratio. For a rotating unbalance, estimate excitation frequency and avoid operating near resonance. For a cam, inspect acceleration/jerk to reduce impact and follower separation.',trick:'External gear meshes reverse direction; internal meshes preserve direction. For a gear train, multiply stage ratios and cancel intermediate tooth counts where possible.',pitfall:'A speed ratio does not tell direction. A flywheel smooths cyclic speed fluctuation; a governor regulates mean speed as load changes. Do not ignore damping near resonance.',visual:'Meshing gears spin in opposite directions; eccentric slider shows changing crank position.'},
{id:'design',name:'Machine Design',group:'Core Mechanical',visual:'design',syllabus:'Design process · Static and fatigue failure · Shafts and keys · Springs · Bolts and joints · Bearings · Gears · Brakes and clutches · Lubrication · Fits, tolerances and reliability',idea:'Convert requirements into loads, choose a failure criterion and material, size the part, then verify fatigue, deflection, manufacturability, safety and cost.',formula:'Von Mises (plane stress): σᵥ=√(σₓ²−σₓσᵧ+σᵧ²+3τₓᵧ²) · Goodman: σₐ/Sₑ+σₘ/Sᵤ≤1/n · Shaft: τ=16T/(πd³)',derive:'For a solid circular shaft, J=πd⁴/32 and outer radius c=d/2. Substitute into τ=Tc/J to obtain τ=16T/(πd³). If bending and torsion act together, combine stresses with a suitable yield/fatigue criterion.',apply:'A shaft design sequence: draw torque and bending diagrams; find critical section; include keyway/shoulder factors; calculate equivalent stress; select diameter; check twist, deflection, fatigue and fit.',trick:'For a circular shaft, strength diameter scales roughly with T^(1/3); stiffness-limited diameter can govern sooner. Compare both. Use a consistent safety-factor definition.',pitfall:'Do not use ultimate strength as a ductile static-yield limit. Endurance strength needs correction for surface, size, reliability, temperature and notch sensitivity.',visual:'Torque twists a shaft; increasing diameter quickly lowers torsional stress.'},
{id:'thermo',name:'Thermodynamics',group:'Thermal Sciences',visual:'thermo',syllabus:'Properties of pure substances · First law · Second law · Entropy · Exergy · Ideal and real gases · Gas and vapour power cycles · Refrigeration · Psychrometry · Combustion',idea:'Choose a system, mark mass/energy crossing its boundary, then write the correct balance. State sign convention and whether values are per unit mass.',formula:'Closed system: Q−W=ΔU · Steady flow: q−wₛ=Δh+Δ(V²/2)+gΔz · η_th=W_net/Q_in · COP_R=Q_L/W_in',derive:'The first law is conservation of energy. For a control volume at steady state, stored energy does not change; inlet and outlet flow carry enthalpy plus kinetic and potential energy. For one inlet/one outlet, this reduces to the steady-flow energy equation.',apply:'Use property tables/software for steam states, identify quality x in the two-phase region, and compare ideal-cycle states to actual component efficiencies. For compressors, track pressure ratio and inlet temperature.',trick:'For an ideal gas, Δu≈c_vΔT and Δh≈c_pΔT. In an isentropic ideal-gas process, T₂/T₁=(p₂/p₁)^((γ−1)/γ). Draw a T-s or p-v sketch before equations.',pitfall:'Heat is not a property stored in a body. Enthalpy is especially useful for flowing systems. Avoid mixing gauge and absolute pressure; use kelvin in gas-law ratios.',visual:'Compression and expansion trace a cycle on a pressure-volume diagram.'},
{id:'fluid',name:'Fluid Mechanics & Machinery',group:'Thermal Sciences',visual:'fluid',syllabus:'Fluid statics · Continuity · Bernoulli and momentum · Dimensional analysis · Laminar/turbulent flow · Pipe losses · Boundary layers · Pumps · Turbines · Compressible flow',idea:'Apply conservation of mass, momentum and energy to a control volume. Account for elevation, pressure, velocity, shaft work and irreversible losses.',formula:'Q=Av · Re=ρVD/μ · Bernoulli: p/ρg+V²/2g+z=constant · Darcy loss: h_f=f(L/D)V²/(2g) · Pump power=ρgQH/η',derive:'For steady incompressible flow, mass in equals mass out, so A₁V₁=A₂V₂. Apply the mechanical energy balance between sections; add pump/turbine head and subtract friction/minor losses to handle real systems.',apply:'At a constriction, continuity increases speed; pressure changes according to the energy balance. For pipe networks, sum head losses around each path and use pump curves at the operating point.',trick:'Reynolds number predicts flow regime, not the friction factor by itself. For laminar circular pipe flow, f_D=64/Re (Darcy convention); Fanning factor is one quarter of Darcy.',pitfall:'Do not mix Darcy and Fanning friction factors. Bernoulli in its simple form omits viscous loss and shaft work. Use absolute pressure for cavitation checks and NPSH.',visual:'Flow accelerates through a narrow throat while pressure head changes.'},
{id:'heat',name:'Heat Transfer',group:'Thermal Sciences',visual:'heat',syllabus:'Conduction · Thermal resistance networks · Fins · Transient conduction · Forced/natural convection · Radiation · Heat exchangers · Boiling and condensation',idea:'Heat transfer is driven by a temperature difference. Identify the mode, geometry, boundary conditions and whether the system is steady or transient.',formula:'Plane wall: Q̇=kAΔT/L · Convection: Q̇=hA(T_s−T∞) · Radiation: Q̇=εσA(T_s⁴−T_sur⁴) · Bi=hL_c/k · NTU=UA/C_min',derive:'Fourier’s law q″=−k dT/dx. For a constant-k plane wall at steady state, integrate across thickness L to get Q̇=kA(T₁−T₂)/L. Resistances in series add just as electrical resistances do.',apply:'Build a resistance network for walls and contact layers; calculate fin efficiency before claiming the fin increases heat transfer. For heat exchangers, choose LMTD with known terminal temperatures or ε–NTU when outlet temperatures are unknown.',trick:'Use a thermal circuit: R_cond=L/(kA), R_conv=1/(hA). If a layer dominates total resistance, improving other layers has little effect.',pitfall:'Radiation temperatures must be absolute kelvin. Biot uses the solid conductivity k and characteristic length; do not confuse it with Reynolds or Fourier number.',visual:'Heat conducts through a wall, then leaves by convection.'},
{id:'manufacturing',name:'Manufacturing & Production',group:'Design and Production',visual:'manufacturing',syllabus:'Casting · Solidification · Forming · Machining · Cutting tools · Welding and joining · Powder metallurgy · Additive manufacturing · CNC · Process planning · Surface finish',idea:'Select a process by material, geometry, tolerance, finish, volume, lead time and cost. Process parameters set both productivity and quality.',formula:'Cutting speed: V=πDN/1000 (D mm, N rpm) · Taylor: VTⁿ=C · MRR(turning)≈πD f d N · Forging load≈flow stress×projected area×constraint factor',derive:'A cutting tool removes a chip as relative motion shears material. Cutting speed sets interface speed, feed controls advance per revolution/stroke, and depth of cut controls engagement. Their combination determines removal rate and tool life.',apply:'Plan datums and operation order so each setup references stable surfaces. For casting, check feeding and solidification shrinkage; for welding, check joint prep, heat input and distortion; for additive, check support, orientation and anisotropy.',trick:'A small increase in cutting speed can sharply reduce tool life under Taylor’s relation; balance tool cost against cycle time. Use process capability to relate tolerance to variation, not only nominal size.',pitfall:'Surface finish, dimensional accuracy and process capability are related but different. Never compare MRRs without matching material, tool, geometry and safety assumptions.',visual:'Turning: the work rotates while a tool advances and removes a chip.'},
{id:'metrology',name:'Metrology, Quality & Reliability',group:'Design and Production',visual:'metrology',syllabus:'Measurement systems · Calibration · Limits and fits · GD&T · Surface texture · Statistical process control · Capability · Acceptance sampling · Reliability · FMEA',idea:'A measurement is an estimate with uncertainty. A process is stable when variation is predictable; capability compares stable process spread with specification width.',formula:'Cp=(USL−LSL)/(6σ) · Cpk=min[(USL−μ)/(3σ),(μ−LSL)/(3σ)] · R(t)=e^(−λt) for constant hazard',derive:'If process output is approximately normal and stable, ±3σ covers about 99.73% of output. Thus Cp divides specification width by 6σ. Cpk additionally measures how near the process mean is to either limit.',apply:'Calibrate against traceable standards, compare measurement uncertainty to tolerance, and use control charts to detect special causes before capability calculations. Use datum references to communicate geometric intent.',trick:'Cp≥Cpk; equality occurs when the process is centered. For reliability of series components, all must work: R_sys=∏R_i. Parallel redundancy works if at least one path survives.',pitfall:'Capability is not a guarantee for an unstable process. Gauge resolution and repeatability can hide real variation. Do not confuse tolerance (allowed) with uncertainty (measurement knowledge).',visual:'A centered process distribution sits within upper and lower specification limits.'},
{id:'industrial',name:'Industrial Engineering & Operations',group:'Design and Production',visual:'industrial',syllabus:'Work study · Time study · Line balancing · Inventory · EOQ · Forecasting · Scheduling · Queuing · Linear programming · Lean · Six Sigma · Supply chain',idea:'Model flow, constraints and variability. Improve the system bottleneck; local utilization does not automatically maximize total throughput.',formula:'EOQ=√(2DS/H) · Little: L=λW · Utilization ρ=λ/μ · Takt time=available production time/customer demand',derive:'For EOQ, annual relevant cost is ordering cost DS/Q plus holding cost HQ/2. Differentiate with respect to Q and set to zero: Q²=2DS/H. The model assumes steady demand, fixed lead time and no shortages.',apply:'Measure cycle time and demand before line balancing. Use ABC analysis to focus inventory control; compute safety stock from demand/lead-time variability; draw a value-stream map to expose waiting and rework.',trick:'For a stable process, Little’s law links work-in-process, throughput and flow time. Reducing WIP often reduces lead time if throughput remains stable.',pitfall:'EOQ ignores quantity discounts, capacity and uncertain demand unless extended. Averages can conceal peaks. In a queue, utilization near 100% can make waiting time grow rapidly.',visual:'A production line’s slowest station limits line throughput.'},
{id:'crosscore',name:'Cross-core Engineering Basics',group:'Cross-core Essentials',visual:'crosscore',syllabus:'DC circuits · Power and energy · Sensors and measurement · Feedback control · First-order response · Basic safety and energy efficiency',idea:'Mechanical systems combine structures, motion, energy, sensors and control. Start with conservation laws and signal direction; keep cross-core analysis proportional to the problem.',formula:'V=IR · P=VI=I²R=V²/R · First-order response: τ=RC or τ=L/R · Feedback error e=r−y',derive:'For a simple resistor, Ohm’s law relates voltage, current and resistance. Electrical power is P=VI; substituting V=IR gives I²R, while substituting I=V/R gives V²/R. A first-order system reaches about 63% of a step change after one time constant.',apply:'A motor converts electrical input to shaft work with losses. A thermocouple or RTD measures temperature; a controller compares measured value with setpoint and adjusts an actuator. Trace power, measurement and feedback paths.',trick:'Use a block diagram before equations: setpoint → controller → actuator → plant → sensor → feedback. Check sensor range, units, polarity and saturation.',pitfall:'Do not equate electrical input power with mechanical output power; account for efficiency. Negative feedback reduces error only when the loop sign and tuning are sensible.',visual:'Feedback compares a setpoint with a sensor measurement and corrects the plant.'},
{id:'cad',name:'Engineering Drawing, CAD & CAE',group:'Design and Production',visual:'cad',syllabus:'Orthographic and sectional views · Fits and tolerances · GD&T · CAD constraints · Assemblies · Materials and mass properties · Mesh quality · Boundary conditions · Verification and validation',idea:'A model is a communication and analysis tool. Define datums, units, load paths, contacts and assumptions so another engineer can reproduce the result.',formula:'Tolerance stack (worst case): T_total=Σ|T_i| · RSS stack (independent): T_RSS=√ΣT_i² · FEA error = discretization + solver + model-form contributions',derive:'For a stack of dimensions, worst-case limits occur when every contributor reaches the sign that maximizes the result. If independent centered variations are acceptable, variances add, leading to the root-sum-square estimate.',apply:'Constrain sketches intentionally; fully define assemblies; document material and contact. In FEA, perform mesh refinement, compare reactions and hand estimates, and validate the model against a test or trusted benchmark.',trick:'Converge quantities of interest, not just a colorful contour. Reaction sums should balance applied loads. Use symmetry only when geometry, loading and constraints are symmetric.',pitfall:'A converged solution can still solve the wrong model. Check units, constraints, singularities, contact status, mesh distortion and idealization limits.',visual:'A constrained sketch becomes a stable parametric model; a refined mesh resolves bending.'}
];

// 2. Expanded Study Guide (expanded-guide.js — 71 lessons)

// Supplementary, source-attributed mini-lessons for the uploaded handouts.
(() => {
 const g=[];
 const add=(parent,group,title,idea,formula,example,method,trap,source)=>g.push({parent,group,title,idea,formula,example,method,trap,source});
 const quant='Student Handout V3 (6) / QA & LR Trainer Handout V3';
 const qa=(module,page)=>`${quant} · ${module} (printed p. ${page})`;
 const sm='Strength of Materials 50 Aptitude Questions, supplied PDF (pp. 1–4)';
 const th='Thermodynamics, supplied PDF (pp. 1–3)';
 const fl='Fluid_Mechanics, supplied PDF (pp. 1–4)';
 const mf='Manufacturing_Process_Aptitude_Test_50_Questions, supplied PDF (pp. 1–8)';
 const md='Machine_Design_Aptitude_Test_50_Questions, supplied PDF (pp. 1–7)';
 const ie='Industrial_Engineering_Basics_Aptitude_Test_50_Questions, supplied PDF (pp. 1–3)';
 add('som','Strength of Materials','Stress, strain and elastic response','Stress is internal force intensity; strain is deformation per original length. In the linear elastic range, stress is proportional to strain.','σ=P/A · ε=δ/L · σ=Eε','A 40 kN load on 500 mm² gives 80 MPa because 1 N/mm² = 1 MPa.','Convert kN to N and keep area in mm²; stress then comes out in MPa.','Do not confuse strength (a limit) with stiffness (E); use the correct area and load direction.',sm);
 add('som','Strength of Materials','Axial extension and thermal stress','A uniform prismatic bar extends under axial load; restrained temperature change can create stress even without an external axial force.','δ=PL/(AE) · δ_T=αLΔT · σ_T=EαΔT (fully restrained)','For 10 kN, L=500 mm, A=200 mm², E=200,000 N/mm²: δ=0.125 mm.','Write all quantities in a consistent N-mm system before substituting.','The free thermal expansion is not stress; restraint creates the thermal stress.',sm);
 add('som','Strength of Materials','Shear force, bending moment and section modulus','Bending stress varies linearly from the neutral axis and is largest at the farthest fiber.','σ=My/I=M/Z · rectangle I=bh³/12 · Z=bh²/6','A simply supported beam with a central point load P has reactions P/2 and Mmax=PL/4.','Draw the free-body diagram, find reactions, then locate the largest moment before calculating stress.','Depth is cubed in I. Use the axis and the corresponding section property.',sm);
 add('som','Strength of Materials','Torsion of circular shafts','Torque produces shear stress and twist. For a solid circular shaft, stress grows with torque and falls sharply as diameter increases.','τmax=16T/(πd³) · θ=TL/(JG) · J_solid=πd⁴/32','Doubling shaft diameter at fixed torque reduces τ to 1/8.','Convert torque to N·mm when diameter is in mm; check allowable stress and angle of twist separately.','Do not use the solid-shaft formula for a hollow shaft; its polar moment differs.',sm);
 add('som','Strength of Materials','Principal stress and failure checks','Plane stress can be reduced to principal stresses; the suitable failure theory depends on material behavior and design purpose.','σ₁,₂=(σx+σy)/2 ± √[((σx−σy)/2)²+τxy²] · σvM=√(σx²−σxσy+σy²+3τ²)','With σx=80 MPa, σy=0 and τ=30 MPa, σvM≈95.4 MPa.','Draw a stress element, use a consistent sign convention, then compare with allowable/design strength.','Theories are models; do not use a ductile yield criterion for brittle fracture without justification.',sm);
 add('thermo','Thermodynamics','System, state and property','A system is the chosen matter or region; state properties describe it. Heat and work are energy transfers across its boundary, not stored properties.','Closed-system energy: ΔU=Q−W (work by system positive)','If a gas receives 500 kJ and does 200 kJ work, ΔU=300 kJ.','State the system boundary and sign convention before writing an energy balance.','Heat and work depend on the process path; internal energy depends on state.',th);
 add('thermo','Thermodynamics','First Law for control volumes','For steady flow, energy carried by mass must be included alongside heat and shaft work.','Q̇−Ẇ=ṁ[(h₂−h₁)+(V₂²−V₁²)/2+g(z₂−z₁)]','Across an insulated turbine with small kinetic/potential changes, shaft output per kg is approximately h₁−h₂.','Sketch inlet/outlet states; decide whether kinetic and potential terms are negligible rather than dropping them automatically.','A closed system and a steady-flow device use different balance forms.',th);
 add('thermo','Thermodynamics','Ideal-gas processes','An ideal gas obeys pV=mRT. Special processes simplify the relation because one state variable or heat/work relation is fixed.','pV=mRT · isothermal: pV=const · isentropic ideal gas: pV^γ=const','For a fixed gas mass at equal temperature, halving volume doubles absolute pressure.','Use absolute pressure and kelvin; identify the process from the wording before choosing an equation.','Gauge pressure cannot be inserted directly in the ideal-gas equation.',th);
 add('thermo','Thermodynamics','Heat engines, refrigerators and COP','An engine converts part of supplied heat into work; a refrigerator uses work to move heat from cold to hot.','η=Wnet/QH=1−QL/QH · COP_R=QL/W · COP_HP=QH/W','QH=1000 kJ, QL=600 kJ gives η=40%.','Draw the heat-flow arrows and label the desired effect before selecting the numerator.','Efficiency is at most 1; refrigerator COP can exceed 1.',th);
 add('thermo','Thermodynamics','Air-standard cycles','Otto, Diesel and Brayton ideal cycles model spark-ignition, compression-ignition and gas-turbine processes, respectively.','Cycle efficiency=net work/heat supplied · Carnot η=1−TL/TH','For reservoirs at 800 K and 300 K, the Carnot limit is 62.5%.','Track each process on p–v or T–s diagrams; compare the ideal result with realistic losses.','Temperature in the Carnot relation must be absolute kelvin.',th);
 add('thermo','Thermodynamics','Steam and refrigeration cycles','Rankine models steam power plants; vapor-compression cycles use compressor, condenser, expansion valve and evaporator.','Rankine: η=Wnet/Qin · refrigeration: COP=Qevap/Win','For a refrigerator with 3 kW cooling and 1 kW input, COP=3.','Follow the working fluid around the cycle and note whether each device adds or removes energy.','An expansion valve is approximately throttling, not an isentropic turbine.',th);
 add('fluid','Fluid Mechanics','Fluid properties and dimensions','Density, viscosity and compressibility govern how a fluid responds to pressure and motion.','ρ=m/V · ν=μ/ρ · specific weight γ=ρg','For μ=0.01 N·s/m² and ρ=1000 kg/m³, ν=1×10⁻⁵ m²/s.','Convert to SI first; distinguish dynamic viscosity μ from kinematic viscosity ν.','The units of ν are m²/s, while μ has units Pa·s.',fl);
 add('fluid','Fluid Mechanics','Hydrostatics and pressure','At rest, pressure rises linearly with depth and acts normal to a surface.','p_g=ρgh · p_abs=p_atm+p_g','At 5 m depth in water, p_g≈49.05 kPa using g=9.81 m/s².','Choose the datum, identify gauge versus absolute pressure, then apply the depth relation.','Pressure is scalar at a point; hydrostatic force on a plane surface acts at the center of pressure.',fl);
 add('fluid','Fluid Mechanics','Continuity and flow rate','Continuity expresses conservation of mass. For steady incompressible flow, flow rate is constant through a streamtube.','Q=AV · A₁V₁=A₂V₂ · for circular pipe, A∝D²','If diameter halves, area quarters and velocity becomes four times at constant flow.','Use diameter squared for circular sections; use area ratio for other shapes.','Continuity alone does not give pressure change; combine it with energy when needed.',fl);
 add('fluid','Fluid Mechanics','Bernoulli equation and head','For ideal steady incompressible flow along a streamline, pressure, velocity and elevation heads trade while total head is constant.','p/(ρg)+V²/(2g)+z=constant','In a horizontal ideal pipe, increasing speed from 2 to 6 m/s lowers static pressure by ρ(6²−2²)/2.','Write stations 1 and 2 and cancel only terms that are truly equal or negligible.','Real pipes have losses and pumps/turbines add or remove head.',fl);
 add('fluid','Fluid Mechanics','Pipe losses and Reynolds number','Viscosity and geometry create friction losses. Reynolds number helps classify internal flow and choose a friction-factor model.','Re=ρVD/μ=VD/ν · h_f=f(L/D)V²/(2g)','Water at V=2 m/s in D=0.05 m with ν=10⁻⁶ m²/s gives Re=100,000.','Calculate Re first; then choose an appropriate laminar or turbulent relation and include minor losses.','The friction factor convention differs between Darcy and Fanning; name the one used.',fl);
 add('fluid','Fluid Mechanics','Pumps and hydraulic power','A pump adds head to fluid; hydraulic power is the useful rate of energy transfer to the flow.','P_h=ρgQH · P_in=P_h/η','Q=0.02 m³/s, H=20 m for water gives P_h≈3.92 kW.','Convert L/s to m³/s and use efficiency only when finding input power.','Do not confuse pump input power with hydraulic output power.',fl);
 add('manufacturing','Manufacturing','Casting: pattern, gating and risers','A pattern forms the mould cavity; the gating system conveys melt and a riser feeds shrinkage during solidification.','Chvorinov: t_s=C(V/A)ⁿ (often n≈2)','A section with lower surface-area-to-volume ratio usually solidifies more slowly.','Trace the metal path: pouring basin → sprue → runner → gate → cavity; place risers to feed hot spots.','Risers feed shrinkage; vents help gases escape. They are not interchangeable.',mf);
 add('manufacturing','Manufacturing','Forming and heat treatment','Rolling, forging, extrusion and drawing shape metal by plastic deformation. Heating changes workability and microstructure.','True strain in upsetting: ε=ln(h₀/h) · reduction r=(h₀−h₁)/h₀','A thickness change from 20 to 15 mm gives a 25% reduction.','Identify the dominant deformation mode and whether the operation is hot or cold.','Cold work can raise strength while reducing ductility; recrystallization depends on material and temperature.',mf);
 add('manufacturing','Manufacturing','Machining and cutting parameters','Cutting speed, feed and depth of cut set tool-work interaction and productivity.','V=πDN/1000 · turning MRR≈πDNfd/1000 (cm³/min with mm units)','For D=50 mm and N=600 rpm, V≈94.2 m/min.','Keep diameter in mm, speed in rpm and apply the unit conversion in the formula.','Tool life, surface finish, force and heat impose limits on productivity.',mf);
 add('manufacturing','Manufacturing','Welding and joining','Welding makes a joint by coalescence; process choice depends on material, thickness, access, production and defect risk.','Heat input per length ≈ ηVI/v','Higher current or voltage and lower travel speed generally increase heat input.','Check joint preparation, process, electrode/filler, shielding and inspection method.','Higher heat input may increase distortion or alter the heat-affected zone; it is not automatically better.',mf);
 add('manufacturing','Manufacturing','CNC and process planning','CNC uses programmed axes and tooling to produce repeatable paths; process planning sequences operations and datums.','Cycle time≈cutting time + handling + tool changes','A part’s setup datum should reference stable, previously machined surfaces where practical.','Plan from functional datums; choose operation sequence, tool, feeds, inspection and safety checks.','CNC repeatability does not remove setup errors, tool wear, fixturing errors or inspection needs.',mf);
 add('manufacturing','Manufacturing','Metrology and least count','A measuring instrument resolves dimensions with finite resolution and uncertainty; calibration relates it to a standard.','Least count=smallest main-scale interval / number of vernier divisions','A 0.5 mm screw pitch with 50 divisions gives 0.01 mm least count.','Choose instrument range and resolution appropriate to tolerance; measure consistently and record calibration status.','Resolution is not accuracy; temperature, force and alignment can bias a reading.',mf);
 add('design','Machine Design','Failure theories and fatigue','Design checks strength, stiffness, wear and cyclic damage with appropriate safety factors and material models.','Factor of safety=n=design strength/working stress · Goodman: σa/Se+σm/Sut≤1','A fully reversed cycle has mean stress zero and alternating stress equal to the peak magnitude.','Separate mean and alternating stress, account for concentration and surface/size factors, then check life or endurance.','A static yield check alone does not establish fatigue life.',md);
 add('design','Machine Design','Shafts, keys and couplings','Shafts transmit torque and often bending; keys transmit torque between shaft and hub.','T=P/ω · τ_solid=16T/(πd³) · key shear force≈2T/d','A 4 kW shaft at 1000 rpm carries T≈38.2 N·m.','Use torque from power and rotational speed, then combine bending and torsion if both are present.','Check shaft deflection, critical speed, key bearing pressure and stress raisers as well as shear stress.',md);
 add('design','Machine Design','Gears and speed ratios','Tooth count and pitch diameter determine the ratio for external spur gears; one external mesh reverses direction.','N₁Z₁=N₂Z₂ · module m=d/Z','A 20-tooth gear at 900 rpm drives a 60-tooth gear at 300 rpm in the opposite direction.','Draw the gear train and count external meshes to determine direction.','Idlers change direction parity and center distance but not the overall magnitude ratio.',md);
 add('design','Machine Design','Springs and bearings','Springs store energy and provide force over deflection; bearings support relative motion and control friction.','Linear spring F=kx · spring index C=D/d','A spring with k=20 N/mm deflected 5 mm produces 100 N.','Check load, deflection, fatigue, solid height and support conditions.','Spring index uses mean coil diameter divided by wire diameter.',md);
 add('industrial','Industrial Engineering','Work study and standard time','Method study improves how work is done; time study measures task time and applies rating and allowances.','Normal time=observed time×rating · standard time=normal time/(1−allowance)','Observed 2 min at 110% rating gives normal time 2.2 min.','Define the method, break work into elements, collect representative observations, then add justified allowance.','Do not compare times when methods, conditions or rating bases differ.',ie);
 add('industrial','Industrial Engineering','Inventory and EOQ','EOQ balances ordering and holding costs under stable demand assumptions.','Q*=√(2DS/H)','D=800 units/year, S=₹50/order, H=₹2/unit-year gives Q*=200 units.','Keep demand and holding cost on the same time basis; inspect the assumptions before using the result.','EOQ does not itself include uncertain demand, quantity discounts or capacity limits.',ie);
 add('industrial','Industrial Engineering','Line balancing and takt','Takt time links available production time to customer demand; a bottleneck limits line throughput.','Takt=available time/demand · minimum stations≥Σtask times/takt','27,000 s available for 450 units gives a takt of 60 s/unit.','List precedence and task times, group feasible tasks, then measure idle time and the bottleneck.','Takt is the demand pace, not the observed cycle time.',ie);
 add('industrial','Industrial Engineering','Quality, process capability and OEE','Control charts identify process change; capability compares a stable process distribution with specification limits.','Cp=(USL−LSL)/(6σ) · Cpk=min[(USL−μ)/(3σ),(μ−LSL)/(3σ)] · OEE=A×P×Q','A width of 1.2 mm and σ=0.1 mm gives Cp=2.','Check stability before interpreting capability; compute the limiting side for Cpk.','Cp ignores centering, while Cpk includes it. Neither repairs an unstable process.',ie);

 const qRows=[
  ['Number system','Integers, factors, primes and remainders form the basis of divisibility and cyclicity problems.','Divisibility by 3 or 9: digit sum; for a remainder, divide and retain the remainder.','For 847, digit sum 19, so it is not divisible by 3.','Reduce powers by cycle length before finding a unit digit.','Do not confuse a factor with a multiple.',qa('Number System',5)],
  ['HCF and LCM','HCF is the greatest common divisor; LCM is the least common multiple.','For two positive integers: HCF×LCM=product.','For 12 and 18: HCF=6, LCM=36.','Use prime factors; take minimum exponents for HCF, maximum for LCM.','The product identity is for two integers, not a general many-number shortcut.',qa('HCF and LCM',11)],
  ['Decimals and fractions','Convert terminating decimals to powers-of-ten fractions and reduce before operating.','a/b ± c/d=(ad±bc)/bd.','0.375=375/1000=3/8.','Cancel common factors before multiplying.','For addition, use a common denominator first.',qa('Decimal Fractions',15)],
  ['Simplification and order of operations','Evaluate brackets, powers, multiplication/division, then addition/subtraction.','BODMAS/PEMDAS; equal-precedence operations proceed left to right.','18÷3×2=6×2=12.','Estimate magnitude first to catch a misplaced operation.','Multiplication does not always come before division.',qa('Simplification',17)],
  ['Percentages and successive change','Percent is a ratio per hundred; sequential changes multiply their factors.','New value=old×(1+p/100).','A 20% rise then 20% fall gives 1.2×0.8=0.96, a 4% fall.','Use a base of 100 or factor multiplication.','Equal up/down percentages do not cancel.',qa('Percentages',20)],
  ['Profit, loss and discounts','Profit/loss compares selling price with cost price; discount compares sale price with marked price.','Profit%=profit/CP×100; discount%=discount/MP×100.','CP ₹800 with 15% profit gives SP ₹920.','Write each percentage’s base next to it.','Do not apply a profit percentage to marked price unless specified.',qa('Profit, Loss and Discounts',24)],
  ['Simple and compound interest','Simple interest is calculated on principal; compound interest reinvests accumulated interest.','SI=Prt/100; A=P(1+r/100)^n.','₹1000 at 10% for 2 years: CI=₹210.','Use year-by-year factors for changing rates.','State whether rates are annual and how often interest compounds.',qa('Simple and Compound Interest',29)],
  ['Ratios, proportions and variation','A ratio compares like quantities; direct variation scales together and inverse variation scales oppositely.','a:b means shares a/(a+b), b/(a+b); y∝x or y∝1/x.','₹700 in 2:5 gives ₹200 and ₹500.','Normalize the total ratio parts before allocating.','Quantities in a ratio must use compatible units.',qa('Ratios, Proportions and Variations',33)],
  ['Partnership','Profit shares follow capital multiplied by the time it is invested.','Share ratio = capital₁×time₁ : capital₂×time₂.','₹10k for 12 months vs ₹15k for 8 months gives equal weights.','Convert all durations to a common unit.','Capital alone is not enough when investment periods differ.',qa('Partnership',37)],
  ['Ages','Age differences remain constant as time passes; sums change with the number of people.','Present age=x; after t years x+t; t years ago x−t.','If A is 4 years older than B, their age difference stays 4.','Translate each sentence into an equation before solving.','Check that a “years ago” age remains physically meaningful.',qa('Ages',40)],
  ['Averages','The arithmetic mean is total divided by count; weighted averages account for group sizes.','Mean=sum/count; combined mean=Σ(nᵢxᵢ)/Σnᵢ.','Average of 4 and 8 is 6; average of two groups depends on their sizes.','Use total = mean×count to replace or add observations.','Do not average group means equally unless group sizes match.',qa('Averages',43)],
  ['Alligation and mixtures','Alligation balances component concentrations or prices around a target average.','Quantity ratio=|higher−mean| : |mean−lower|.','Mix 20% and 50% material for 30%: ratio 20:10 = 2:1.','Place the target mean between the two strengths and cross-subtract.','Keep concentration units and total quantity consistent.',qa('Alligations and Mixtures',47)],
  ['Time and work','Work rates add for independent workers working together.','Rate=1/time; combined time=1/(Σ rates).','A takes 6 days, B 3 days: together 1/(1/6+1/3)=2 days.','Use total work as an LCM when rates include fractions.','Do not add completion times; add rates.',qa('Time and Work',51)],
  ['Speed, distance and time','Average speed is total distance divided by total time.','d=vt; average speed=Σd/Σt.','Equal distances at 30 and 60 km/h average 40 km/h.','For equal distances, harmonic mean is a quick check.','Arithmetic mean speed is wrong when travel times differ.',qa('Time, Speed and Distance',57)],
  ['Trains, boats and streams','Relative speed combines velocities; current changes a boat’s ground speed.','Opposite directions: v₁+v₂; same: |v₁−v₂|; boat downstream=u+v, upstream=u−v.','Train length 120 m at 15 m/s passes a pole in 8 s.','Use relative speed and the full distance that must clear the object.','Convert km/h to m/s with ×5/18 when lengths are metres.',qa('Trains, Boats and Streams, Races',62)],
  ['Permutation and combination','Permutation counts ordered selections; combination counts unordered selections.','nPr=n!/(n−r)!; nCr=n!/[r!(n−r)!].','Choose 2 from 5: 5C2=10.','Ask whether changing order creates a different outcome.','Do not multiply by r! for an unordered selection.',qa('Permutation and Combination',68)],
  ['Probability','For equally likely outcomes, probability is favorable outcomes divided by total outcomes.','P(A)=n(A)/n(S); complement P(not A)=1−P(A).','3 red among 8 counters gives P(red)=3/8.','Use complements for “at least one” events.','For dependent draws without replacement, update the sample space.',qa('Probability',75)],
  ['Data interpretation','Tables and charts encode counts, ratios, rates and percentage changes.','% change=(new−old)/old×100.','Output rises from 80 to 100: increase=25%.','Read units and footnotes before comparing; compute only what is asked.','Percentage-point change differs from percent change.',qa('Data Interpretation',81)],
  ['Mensuration','Area and volume formulas depend on shape and dimension; scaling length scales area by square and volume by cube.','Circle A=πr²; cylinder V=πr²h; rectangle A=lb.','Doubling a cylinder radius at fixed height quadruples volume.','Sketch and label radii, diameters and heights.','Do not substitute diameter where radius is required.',qa('Mensuration',86)],
  ['Blood relations','Represent each relation from the speaker’s point of view, then link the chain.','Draw a small family tree with generations and gender labels.','“My mother’s son” may be the speaker or a brother; use context.','Resolve one relation at a time; mark pronouns explicitly.','Do not assume the speaker’s gender or a unique sibling identity.',qa('Blood Relations',92)],
  ['Direction sense','Track turns as coordinates; opposite directions differ by 180°.','Net displacement=√(east²+north²).','3 km north and 4 km east gives 5 km direct distance.','Draw N/E axes and update heading after every turn.','Path length is not the same as displacement.',qa('Direction Sense Test',98)],
  ['Series','Find a consistent rule in differences, ratios, alternating terms or position-based patterns.','Arithmetic: aₙ=a₁+(n−1)d; geometric: aₙ=a₁rⁿ⁻¹.','4, 7, 10, 13 has difference 3, so next is 16.','Check first differences, then second differences or alternating streams.','Do not overfit a complex rule when a simple repeated rule works.',qa('Series',101)],
  ['Coding and decoding','Codes transform letters or words using a consistent mapping or operation.','A→1,…,Z→26 is one common mapping; shift rules wrap at 26.','A + 2 letters becomes C.','Compare several encoded pairs to infer a rule, then test it on every pair.','One example rarely proves a unique code.',qa('Coding and Decoding',107)],
  ['Analogy','An analogy preserves a relation between two pairs.','A:B :: C:?; state the relation in a short verb phrase.','Thermometer:temperature :: barometer:pressure.','Name the relation before selecting the option.','Avoid choosing by surface similarity alone.',qa('Analogy',111)],
  ['Seating arrangement','Position constraints define a set of possible arrangements; solve fixed relations before flexible ones.','For n people in a row there are n! raw arrangements before constraints.','If A is immediately left of B, keep the pair together as AB.','Draw seats and use a table for each clue; branch only when necessary.','“Left of” depends on whether the person faces inward or outward.',qa('Seating Arrangement',114)],
  ['Data arrangement and sufficiency','Arrangement puzzles combine constraints; data sufficiency asks if given facts uniquely answer a question.','Test each statement alone, then both together.','A=x+2 and B=x−1 gives A−B=3 without knowing x.','Separate solving the question from evaluating information sufficiency.','A statement can be enough even when it does not give every variable.',qa('Data Arrangement / Data Sufficiency',119)],
  ['Clocks and calendars','Clock hands move at different angular speeds; calendar questions use day remainders.','Minute hand=6°/min; hour hand=0.5°/min.','At 3:00 the hands differ by 90°.','Reduce elapsed days modulo 7 for weekday shifts.','Account for leap years only when the interval crosses the relevant February.',qa('Clocks / Calendars',124)],
  ['Syllogisms','A conclusion must follow from the stated sets; do not add outside assumptions.','Use Venn regions for all/some/no statements.','All pumps are machines; some machines are portable does not prove some pumps are portable.','Check whether a conclusion is true in every diagram satisfying the premises.','“Some” does not mean “all,” and existence is not implied by “all” alone.',qa('Syllogisms',132)],
  ['Cryptarithmetic','Each letter stands for one digit; equal letters match and different letters use different digits. Leading letters of multi-digit numbers cannot be zero.','Solve column by column with carries; enforce digit uniqueness after each assignment.','For SEND+MORE=MONEY, the units column constrains D+E to end in Y; carries then constrain the next columns.','Start at the units column, track carries, and use the leading-digit and no-repeat rules to prune choices.','Do not assign letters independently after arithmetic has already fixed their possible values.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Cryptarithmetic (printed p. 155); QA & LR Trainer Handout V3 (printed p. 224)`],
  ['Cubes and visual reasoning','Track painted faces and rotations by position, not by imagined viewpoint.','A cube cut into n³ equal cubes has corner pieces with 3 painted faces.','For n=3, the center small cube has no painted faces.','Count corners, edges, face centers and interior separately.','Rotation changes orientation but not adjacency or number of painted faces.',qa('Cubes / Visual Reasoning',144)],
  ['Articles and prepositions','Articles signal specificity; prepositions show relations such as place, time and direction.','Use “a” before a consonant sound, “an” before a vowel sound, “the” for a specific referent.','“An hour” uses the vowel sound; “a university” begins with a consonant /y/ sound.','Read aloud for sound, then check whether the noun is general or specific.','Choose by pronunciation, not the first written letter.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 158–163)`],
  ['Tenses and subject–verb agreement','Verb form must agree with the subject and time; intervening phrases do not change the subject.','Singular present: he/she/it + verb-s; plural subjects use base verb.','“The set of readings is complete.” Subject is “set.”','Locate the main subject, identify time, then select the verb form.','A nearby plural noun can distract from a singular head noun.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 164–168)`],
  ['Voice and reported speech','Active voice foregrounds the doer; passive voice foregrounds the receiver. Reported speech shifts pronouns and sometimes tense.','Passive: object + suitable be + past participle (+ by-agent).','“The operator checked the valve” → “The valve was checked by the operator.”','Preserve tense, meaning and time references while transforming the sentence.','Do not change tense mechanically when the reporting context keeps the fact current.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 169–174)`],
  ['Error spotting and sentence correction','Check agreement, tense, parallel structure, modifiers, pronouns and idiomatic usage.','Parallel items should share grammatical form.','“Measure, record and report” keeps three verbs parallel.','Read the full sentence after each edit; check meaning and grammar together.','A sentence can have multiple grammatical clauses but still be unclear.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 175–182)`],
  ['Synonyms, antonyms and verbal analogy','Word questions test meaning in context, degree and relation.','Replace the word in its sentence and test whether tone and meaning remain.','“Mitigate” means reduce severity; it does not mean eliminate.','Use the sentence context before relying on a memorized synonym.','Near-synonyms can differ in intensity or usage.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 183–189)`],
  ['Sentence completion and one-word substitution','Choose the word or phrase that completes both grammar and logic.','Check part of speech, collocation and contrast/cause clues.','“Despite the delay, the team completed the trial” signals contrast.','Read the whole sentence with each option inserted.','A grammatically fitting word may still contradict the passage logic.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 190–195)`],
  ['Para jumbles and cloze tests','Coherent order follows references, chronology, cause, contrast and topic sentences.','Pronouns generally follow their referents; connectors reveal logical direction.','A sentence beginning “Therefore” usually depends on an earlier reason.','Find the opening sentence and mandatory pairs before ordering the rest.','Do not rely on a single connector when the passage has several clues.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 196–204)`],
  ['Reading comprehension','Answer from the passage; distinguish explicit facts, inference, tone and main idea.','Main idea should cover the passage without adding a new claim.','If a passage says a test “may reduce risk,” do not infer it eliminates risk.','Read the question, locate its evidence, then eliminate unsupported options.','Outside knowledge can be true but irrelevant to what the author states.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 205–212)`],
  ['Critical reasoning','Arguments contain premises and a conclusion; evaluate assumptions, evidence and alternative explanations.','An assumption is a needed bridge from evidence to conclusion.','A pump passed one test; that alone does not prove lifetime reliability.','Identify the conclusion first, then ask what must be true for the evidence to support it.','Correlation, one example or a stated possibility is not proof of causation.',`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 213–220)`]
 ];
 qRows.forEach(([title,idea,formula,example,method,trap,source])=>add('aptitude','Aptitude & Reasoning',title,idea,formula,example,method,trap,source));
 add('aptitude','Aptitude & Reasoning','Train problems · length, platforms and relative speed','The distance a train must cover is the distance from its front reaching the object until its rear clears it. A pole has negligible length; a platform or tunnel does not.','Distance cleared = train length + object length · time = distance / relative speed · km/h to m/s: ×5/18 · opposite directions: add speeds · same direction: subtract speeds','Example 1: 60 km/h = 16⅔ m/s. Passing a pole in 9 s gives train length = 16⅔×9 = 150 m. Example 2: a 125 m train passes a man walking at 5 km/h in the same direction in 10 s. Relative speed = 125/10 = 12.5 m/s = 45 km/h, so train speed = 45+5 = 50 km/h.','1) Draw the direction arrows. 2) Convert speeds to m/s when using metres and seconds. 3) Use L for a pole/person, L+platform for a platform/tunnel, and L₁+L₂ for two trains. 4) Divide that distance by relative speed. 5) Convert back to km/h only if asked.','Same-direction motion uses the speed difference; opposite-direction motion uses the sum. Do not use only the train length when clearing a platform or another train.','Student Handout V3 (6), Trains, Boats and Streams, Races (printed p. 62).');
 window.MECHPREP_EXTRA_GUIDE=g;
})();

// 3. Semester 3 Study Guide (semester3-guide.js — 20 lessons)

// Supplemental lessons based on the user's third-semester Drive course files.
(() => {
  const g=[];
  const add=(parent,group,title,idea,formula,example,method,trap,source)=>g.push({parent,group,title,idea,formula,example,method,trap,source});
  const km='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME310 KOM/QP/KOM QBANK ANS.pdf';
  const fm='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME320 FM/QP/FLUID-MECHANICS-question-Bank.pdf';
  const ff='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME36L WS/Foundry-and-Forging-Laboratory-Manual (1).pdf';
  const ms='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME37L MS/Laboratory-Manual-Machine-Shop-1.pdf';
  const camd='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME350B CAMD/QUESTION PAPER.pdf';
  const mom='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME340 MOM/QP/MOM QBANK ANS.pdf';
  const dip='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22MATDIP310 M1/unit 3.pdf';
  const btd='G:/My Drive/3rd sem/22ME330 BTD/QP/BTD QBANK ANS.pdf';
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','KOM Unit I · mechanisms and mobility','Name links, kinematic pairs, chains and mechanisms before calculating mobility. For a planar lower-pair chain, count links and one-degree-of-freedom pairs explicitly.','Gruebler: M=3(L−1)−2J₁−J₂','A four-link four-revolute chain gives M=3(4−1)−2(4)=1.','Count the frame as a link; classify each joint, then check whether special geometry changes the generic mobility result.','Gruebler is a generic criterion; coincident constraints or special link geometry can make the actual mobility differ.',km+' · Unit I, printed pp. 2–49');
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','Inversions and Grashof condition','An inversion fixes a different link of the same kinematic chain. Grashof’s inequality predicts whether at least one link can rotate fully in a four-bar chain.','S+L ≤ P+Q (Grashof); S+L > P+Q (non-Grashof)','For lengths 20, 40, 50, 70 mm: 20+70=90 ≤ 40+50=90, so the chain is Grashof.','Sort shortest S and longest L, compare against the other two links, then state which link is fixed to identify the inversion.','Equality is the change-point case; the inequality alone does not name the fixed-link mechanism.',km+' · Unit I, printed pp. 2–49');
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','Quick-return mechanisms','A quick-return mechanism has unequal forward and return times because its crank sweeps unequal angles at constant speed.','Time ratio=crank angle for cutting / crank angle for return','At constant crank speed, strokes spanning 220° and 140° have time ratio 220/140=1.571.','Identify which stroke is cutting, measure the corresponding crank angles, and divide; angular speed cancels.','Do not invert the ratio: state clearly whether it is cutting/return or return/cutting.',km+' · Unit I, printed pp. 2–49');
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','KOM Unit II · velocity and acceleration','Velocity directions follow the instantaneous geometry; relative velocity relates points on a rigid link. Acceleration has tangential and normal components.','v_B=v_A+ω×r_BA · a_t=αr · a_n=ω²r toward center','A point 0.12 m from a pivot rotating at 10 rad/s has normal acceleration 12 m/s² inward.','Draw the link, mark known directions, write vector components, then solve the velocity or acceleration polygon/equations.','Centripetal acceleration points toward the pivot; angular acceleration creates a tangential component.',km+' · Unit II, printed pp. 50–95');
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','KOM Unit III · cam motion','Cam displacement laws define follower rise, dwell and return. Smooth motion reduces impact; acceleration and jerk matter beyond just displacement.','For simple harmonic rise h over cam angle β: y=(h/2)[1−cos(πθ/β)]','At half rise angle θ=β/2, y=h/2.','Break one revolution into rise/dwell/return intervals; use the specified law for each interval and check boundary continuity.','A displacement diagram is not enough to assess vibration; inspect velocity, acceleration and jerk too.',km+' · Unit III, printed pp. 96–144');
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','KOM Unit IV · gear trains','A gear pair trades speed for torque ideally. For external gears, directions reverse; a compound train multiplies stage ratios.','N₁T₁=N₂T₂ · train ratio=product(driver teeth/driven teeth)','A 20-tooth gear at 600 rpm drives a 60-tooth gear: output speed 200 rpm, opposite direction.','Mark driver/driven for each mesh, multiply speed ratios, and count direction reversals separately.','Idler gears change direction or spacing but cancel from the magnitude ratio in a simple train.',km+' · Unit IV, printed pp. 145–208');
  add('tom','Semester 3 · Kinematics of Machines','KOM Unit V · friction in machine elements','Friction influences power loss, wear and self-locking in screws, bearings, belts and clutches. Choose a model that matches the contact geometry.','Friction angle: tan φ=μ · limiting friction F=μN','For μ=0.20, φ=tan⁻¹(0.20)=11.31°.','Draw the contact forces and impending motion first; distinguish static friction from kinetic friction and bearing/collar friction models.','Friction opposes relative or impending slip at the interface; it is not always opposite the object’s velocity.',km+' · Unit V, printed pp. 209–273');
  add('fluid','Semester 3 · Fluid Mechanics','FM Chapter I · fluid properties','Density and specific weight describe mass and weight per volume. Dynamic and kinematic viscosity describe resistance to shear and its density-normalized form.','ρ=m/V · γ=ρg · ν=μ/ρ','For μ=0.001 Pa·s and ρ=1000 kg/m³, ν=1×10⁻⁶ m²/s.','Write SI units beside each value; convert centipoise/poise carefully before substitution.','Dynamic viscosity μ has units Pa·s; kinematic viscosity ν has units m²/s.',fm+' · Chapter I, pp. 1–7');
  add('fluid','Semester 3 · Fluid Mechanics','FM Chapter II · pressure measurement','A manometer balances pressure through hydrostatic head differences. Walk from one point to another and add pressure when moving down in a fluid.','Δp=ρgΔh','A 0.30 m water-column difference gives Δp≈1000×9.81×0.30=2.94 kPa.','Start at a known pressure, trace the connected columns, apply each fluid density and elevation change, then solve for the unknown.','Use the density of the fluid actually traversed in each column; distinguish gauge from absolute pressure.',fm+' · Chapter II, pp. 8–14');
  add('fluid','Semester 3 · Fluid Mechanics','FM Chapter III · hydrostatic force','Pressure increases with depth; the resultant on a plane submerged surface acts at the centre of pressure, below the centroid for a vertical plane.','F=ρg h̄ A · h_cp=h̄+I_G/(h̄A) (vertical plane)','For a vertical rectangle, the pressure resultant is deeper than its centroid because pressure is larger at the bottom.','Find centroid depth h̄ and area A for force; use the second moment I_G to locate the centre of pressure.','h̄ is centroid depth and h_cp is centre-of-pressure depth; do not substitute one for the other.',fm+' · Chapter III, pp. 15–19');
  add('fluid','Semester 3 · Fluid Mechanics','FM Chapter IV · kinematics and Bernoulli','Continuity conserves mass. Bernoulli relates pressure, velocity and elevation head along an ideal streamline; real systems add losses and machine head.','A₁V₁=A₂V₂ · p/(ρg)+V²/(2g)+z=constant','A pipe narrows to half its area: for incompressible steady flow, its velocity doubles.','Write continuity first; then apply an energy balance between sections and include pump/turbine head and losses when present.','Simple Bernoulli assumes negligible viscous losses and no shaft work between the points.',fm+' · Chapter IV, pp. 20–28');
  add('fluid','Semester 3 · Fluid Mechanics','FM Chapter V · pipe flow','Pipe head loss depends on friction, length-to-diameter ratio and velocity head. Minor losses represent fittings and area changes.','h_f=f_D(L/D)V²/(2g) · h_m=K V²/(2g)','Doubling mean velocity makes a fixed Darcy-Weisbach loss four times larger if f_D remains constant.','Choose Darcy or Fanning convention, compute Reynolds number, determine the applicable friction factor, then add minor losses.','Darcy friction factor is four times the Fanning factor; do not mix the two.',fm+' · Chapter V, pp. 29–31');
  add('manufacturing','Semester 3 · Foundry and Forging Lab','Foundry lab · sand quality tests','Moulding sand must retain shape, vent gases and resist handling/metal pressure. Lab tests assess strength, permeability, moisture/clay and grain size.','Permeability compares gas-flow capacity under a specified pressure/head and specimen geometry.','If gas cannot escape the mould, gas defects such as blows or pinholes can form in the casting.','For each test, record specimen preparation, machine setting, readings and units; compare samples only under the same procedure.','A test result is meaningful only with the sample condition and procedure documented.',ff+' · Unit 1; printed pp. 4–35');
  add('manufacturing','Semester 3 · Foundry and Forging Lab','Foundry practice · mould preparation','Mould preparation uses patterns, cope/drag boxes, parting sand, gates, runners, risers and vents to shape and feed a casting.','Casting layout: pouring basin → sprue → runner → ingate; riser feeds solidification shrinkage.','A riser should remain liquid longer than the casting region it feeds.','Plan parting, pattern withdrawal, gating and riser before ramming; verify mould alignment and safe pouring practice.','A gate conveys metal; a riser supplies liquid metal during shrinkage. They are not interchangeable.',ff+' · Unit 1, Foundry Practice; printed pp. 37–65');
  add('manufacturing','Semester 3 · Foundry and Forging Lab','Forging operations and lab models','Forging plastically deforms heated metal using compressive force. Upsetting increases section and shortens length; drawing out does the opposite.','Approximate volume constancy: A₁L₁≈A₂L₂','If bar length is reduced to 0.8L by upsetting, average area rises to about A/0.8=1.25A.','Identify the intended shape change, heat uniformly to the specified range, then use controlled blows and measure the finished dimensions.','Volume constancy is an ideal approximation; scale, flash, temperature and material flow cause real deviations.',ff+' · Unit 2; printed pp. 66–79');
  add('manufacturing','Semester 3 · Machine Shop Lab','Machine shop · cutting speed and setup','Turning, shaping and milling require a suitable tool, workholding, speed, feed and depth of cut. Rigidity and safe setup govern achievable accuracy.','Turning speed V=πDN/1000 (D in mm, N rpm); N=1000V/(πD)','For D=50 mm and V=100 m/min, N≈637 rpm.','Choose material/tool data, calculate a safe spindle speed, clamp securely, set tool height/clearance, and inspect dimensions during machining.','The manual is labeled V Semester on its cover, so verify whether it applies to this third-semester course.',ms+' · Machine Shop Laboratory Manual, printed cover identifies V Semester');
  add('math','Semester 3 · Applied Mathematics','Taylor expansion · one-variable approximation','Taylor expansion approximates a smooth function near a point using derivatives there. Maclaurin is the special case centered at zero.','f(a+h)=Σ[f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)/n!]hⁿ','For ln x about x=1, let h=x−1: ln x≈h−h²/2+h³/3−h⁴/4.','Set h=x−a, evaluate derivatives at a, substitute the required h and truncate at the requested degree.','A Taylor polynomial is local; its accuracy depends on distance from the expansion point and the function’s convergence behavior.',dip+' · Unit 3, scanned handwritten notes; example visible on first rendered page');
  add('manufacturing','Semester 3 · Computer Aided Machine Drawing','CAMD exam drawing workflow','The supplied paper combines projection/sectioning, orthographic views, fasteners, joints and a major assembly drawing. Set views and conventions before detailing.','Paper pattern shown: 15 + 15 + 15 + 15 + 40 = 100 marks; 3 hours.','Reserve substantial time for the 40-mark assembly question and leave a final check for dimensions, alignment and hidden edges.','Begin with centerlines and principal axes; construct primary geometry, project related views, add sections/threads, then dimension and check line conventions.','This is a drawing paper; the app’s MCQ practice cannot reproduce hands-on CAD construction.',camd+' · question paper, first page / paper details');
  add('som','Semester 3 · Mechanics of Materials','MOM exam short answers · elastic constants','Elastic constants relate normal/shear stress to strain in idealized elastic response; factor of safety compares capacity with design demand.','E=σ/ε · G=τ/γ · ν=−ε_lateral/ε_longitudinal','A 100 MPa stress causing strain 0.0005 gives E=200 GPa.','State the loading and strain definition; use consistent units and distinguish Young’s modulus, shear modulus and Poisson ratio.','The scanned bank’s first page confirms short-answer definitions, but this lesson is a foundational recap, not a transcription.',mom+' · scanned question bank, first page');
  add('thermo','Semester 3 · Basic Thermodynamics','BTD paper structure and energy balance','The supplied exam paper is organized into five modules and instructs students to answer one full question from each module. The visible first page tests fundamentals and the first law.','Paper header: 3 hours, 100 marks; five modules. Closed system: Q−W=ΔU (work by system positive).','A closed system receiving 500 kJ heat and doing 200 kJ work has ΔU=300 kJ.','Identify system boundary, sign convention and whether the process is closed or steady-flow before choosing an energy equation.','Only visible exam structure and page-one concepts are used here; the scanned paper’s unreadable later pages are not inferred.',btd+' · scanned question bank, first page and visible exam instructions');
  window.MECHPREP_EXTRA_GUIDE=(window.MECHPREP_EXTRA_GUIDE||[]).concat(g);
})();

// 4. Core Generated Question Bank V1 (extra-question-bank.js — 520 questions)

// Deterministic, locally generated practice set: 360 mechanical + 160 aptitude MCQs.
// Every variant has a worked calculation; there are no network calls or API keys here.
(() => {
  const questions=[];
  const levels=['Basic','Intermediate','Advanced'];
  const byTrack={
    'Engineering Mechanics':'Mechanical Core','Strength of Materials':'CAE / Analysis',
    'Machine Design':'CAD / Design','Theory of Machines':'CAD / Design',
    'Thermodynamics':'Maintenance / Utilities','Fluid Mechanics':'Maintenance / Utilities',
    'Heat Transfer':'Maintenance / Utilities','Manufacturing':'Manufacturing',
    'Metrology':'Quality / Metrology','Industrial Engineering':'Supply Chain / Production',
    'Cross-core Engineering Basics':'Mechanical + Aptitude'
  };
  const round=(n,d=1)=>Number(n.toFixed(d));
  const fmt=(n,d=1)=>Number(n).toFixed(d).replace(/\.0$/,'');
  function seededSort(options,id){
    let h=0;for(const c of id)h=(h*31+c.charCodeAt(0))>>>0;
    return options.map((v,i)=>({v,k:Math.sin(h+i*17.123)*10000})).sort((a,b)=>a.k-b.k).map(x=>x.v);
  }
  function addChoice({id,category='mechanical',topic,difficulty,role,prompt,correct,wrong,explain}){
    const options=seededSort([correct,...wrong.filter(x=>x!==correct)].slice(0,4),id);
    if(new Set(options).size<4)throw new Error(`Duplicate options in ${id}`);
    questions.push({id,category,role:role||byTrack[topic]||'Mechanical Core',topic,difficulty,text:prompt,options,answer:options.indexOf(correct),explain});
  }
  function addNum({id,category='mechanical',topic,difficulty,role,prompt,value,unit='',decimals=1,step,explain}){
    const target=round(value,decimals), delta=step||Math.max(10**(-decimals),Math.abs(target)*0.12);
    const candidates=[target+delta,target-delta,target+2*delta,target/2,target*2,target+4*delta,target-3*delta]
      .map(v=>round(v,decimals)).filter(v=>Number.isFinite(v)&&v!==target);
    const wrong=[...new Set(candidates)].slice(0,3);
    while(wrong.length<3){const next=round(target+(wrong.length+5)*delta,decimals);if(next!==target&&!wrong.includes(next))wrong.push(next);}
    const renderValue=v=>unit==='₹'?`₹${fmt(v,decimals)}`:unit==='%'?`${fmt(v,decimals)}%`:unit?.startsWith('₹/')?`₹${fmt(v,decimals)}${unit.slice(1)}`:`${fmt(v,decimals)}${unit?` ${unit}`:''}`;
    addChoice({id,category,topic,difficulty,role,prompt,correct:renderValue(target),wrong:wrong.map(renderValue),explain});
  }
  function addFamily(name,topic,fn){for(let i=0;i<15;i++)fn(i,levels[(i+name.length)%3],`${name}-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`);}

  // Mechanical core: 24 families × 15 distinct parameter sets = 360 questions.
  addFamily('moment','Engineering Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const F=120+24*i,arm=.25+.05*(i%6),M=round(F*arm,1);addNum({id,topic:'Engineering Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A ${F} N force acts perpendicular to a ${arm.toFixed(2)} m lever arm. What moment acts about the pivot?`,value:M,unit:'N·m',decimals:1,step:Math.max(1,round(F*.05,1)),explain:`For a perpendicular force, M=F r. Thus M=${F}×${arm.toFixed(2)}=${M} N·m.`});});
  addFamily('axial-stress','Strength of Materials',(i,d,id)=>{const F=10+5*i,A=100+25*(i%5),v=F*1000/A;addNum({id,topic:'Strength of Materials',difficulty:d,prompt:`A uniform bar carries ${F} kN in tension over an area of ${A} mm². Find the normal stress.`,value:v,unit:'MPa',decimals:0,step:10,explain:`σ=F/A. Since 1 N/mm²=1 MPa, σ=(${F}×1000)/${A}=${fmt(v,0)} MPa.`});});
  addFamily('axial-elongation','Strength of Materials',(i,d,id)=>{const P=5+5*i,L=300+50*(i%8),A=100+20*(i%4),E=200,v=P*L/(A*E);addNum({id,topic:'Strength of Materials',difficulty:d,prompt:`A steel rod (${A} mm², ${L} mm long) carries ${P} kN tension. Take E=200 GPa. Estimate elongation.`,value:v,unit:'mm',decimals:3,step:.025,explain:`δ=FL/(AE). With E=200,000 N/mm², δ=(${P}×1000×${L})/(${A}×200000)=${v.toFixed(3)} mm.`});});
  addFamily('beam-bending','Strength of Materials',(i,d,id)=>{const b=24,h=40+4*(i%8),sigma=10+5*i,M=sigma*b*h*h/6;addNum({id,topic:'Strength of Materials',difficulty:d,prompt:`A rectangular beam (b=${b} mm, h=${h} mm) has I=bh³/12. If M=${M.toLocaleString('en-US')} N·mm, find the maximum bending stress.`,value:sigma,unit:'MPa',decimals:0,step:5,explain:`σ=Mc/I. Here c=h/2 and I=bh³/12, so σ=6M/(bh²)=6×${M}/(${b}×${h}²)=${sigma} MPa.`});});
  addFamily('shaft-torsion','Strength of Materials',(i,d,id)=>{const dia=20+2*(i%8),target=10+4*i,T=round(target*Math.PI*dia**3/16,0),actual=16*T/(Math.PI*dia**3);addNum({id,topic:'Strength of Materials',difficulty:d,prompt:`A solid shaft of diameter ${dia} mm transmits ${fmt(T/1000,2)} N·m torque. Estimate maximum shear stress.`,value:actual,unit:'MPa',decimals:1,step:2,explain:`For a solid circular shaft, τmax=16T/(πd³). Convert torque to N·mm: ${T} N·mm. Substitution gives τ≈${actual.toFixed(1)} MPa.`});});
  addFamily('beam-reactions','Engineering Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const P=2+2*i,R=P/2;addNum({id,topic:'Engineering Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A simply supported beam carries a ${P} kN point load at mid-span. What is the vertical reaction at each support?`,value:R,unit:'kN',decimals:0,step:1,explain:`The geometry is symmetric, so reactions are equal. From ΣFy=0, 2R=${P}; therefore each reaction is ${R} kN.`});});
  addFamily('gear-speed','Theory of Machines',(i,d,id)=>{const z1=18+2*(i%6),ratio=2+(i%3),z2=z1*ratio,N1=300+30*i,N2=N1/ratio,correct=`${N2} rpm, opposite direction`;const wrong=[`${N1*ratio} rpm, opposite direction`,`${N2} rpm, same direction`,`${N1} rpm, opposite direction`];addChoice({id,topic:'Theory of Machines',difficulty:d,prompt:`An external ${z1}-tooth gear at ${N1} rpm drives a ${z2}-tooth gear. What is the driven speed and direction?`,correct,wrong,explain:`N₁Z₁=N₂Z₂, so N₂=${N1}×${z1}/${z2}=${N2} rpm. A single external mesh reverses direction.`});});
  addFamily('belt-speed','Theory of Machines',(i,d,id)=>{const D1=10+(i%6),ratio=2+(i%3),D2=D1*ratio,N1=240+24*i,N2=N1/ratio;addNum({id,topic:'Theory of Machines',difficulty:d,prompt:`A ${D1} cm driver pulley rotates at ${N1} rpm and drives a ${D2} cm pulley with no slip. Find the driven speed.`,value:N2,unit:'rpm',decimals:0,step:12,explain:`With no slip, N₁D₁=N₂D₂. Thus N₂=${N1}×${D1}/${D2}=${N2} rpm.`});});
  addFamily('first-law','Thermodynamics',(i,d,id)=>{const Q=150+40*i,W=30+10*(i%9),U=Q-W;addNum({id,topic:'Thermodynamics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A closed system receives ${Q} kJ heat and does ${W} kJ work. Find ΔU, taking work by the system as positive.`,value:U,unit:'kJ',decimals:0,step:10,explain:`For a closed system, ΔU=Q−W=${Q}−${W}=${U} kJ.`});});
  addFamily('sensible-heat','Thermodynamics',(i,d,id)=>{const m=1+i%5,cp=round(.8+.2*(i%8),1),dt=10+5*i,Q=round(m*cp*dt,1);addNum({id,topic:'Thermodynamics',difficulty:d,prompt:`${m} kg of a solid (cp=${cp} kJ/(kg·K)) is heated by ${dt} K. Estimate heat supplied.`,value:Q,unit:'kJ',decimals:1,step:Math.max(1,round(Q*.15,1)),explain:`Q=mcₚΔT=${m}×${cp}×${dt}=${Q.toFixed(1)} kJ, assuming constant specific heat and no phase change.`});});
  addFamily('carnot','Thermodynamics',(i,d,id)=>{const Th=500+50*i,Tc=200+10*(i%16),eta=(1-Tc/Th)*100;addNum({id,topic:'Thermodynamics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A reversible engine operates between ${Th} K and ${Tc} K reservoirs. Find its maximum thermal efficiency.`,value:eta,unit:'%',decimals:1,step:2,explain:`Carnot efficiency ηmax=1−T_L/T_H=1−${Tc}/${Th}=${eta.toFixed(1)}%.`});});
  addFamily('continuity','Fluid Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const A=5+i,V=2+(i%6),Q=A*V*.1;addNum({id,topic:'Fluid Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`Flow crosses a ${A} cm² section at ${V} m/s. Find volume flow rate in L/s.`,value:Q,unit:'L/s',decimals:1,step:.5,explain:`Q=Av. Since 1 cm²=10⁻⁴ m² and 1 m³/s=1000 L/s, Q=${A}×${V}×0.1=${Q.toFixed(1)} L/s.`});});
  addFamily('reynolds','Fluid Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const V=round(.5+.1*i,1),D=round(.02+.005*(i%8),3),rho=1000,mu=.001,Re=rho*V*D/mu;addNum({id,topic:'Fluid Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`Water (ρ=1000 kg/m³, μ=0.001 Pa·s) flows at ${V} m/s in a ${D} m pipe. Find Reynolds number.`,value:Re,unit:'',decimals:0,step:5000,explain:`Re=ρVD/μ=1000×${V}×${D}/0.001=${fmt(Re,0)}. It is dimensionless.`});});
  addFamily('hydrostatic','Fluid Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const h=round(.5+.25*i,2),p=1000*10*h/1000;addNum({id,topic:'Fluid Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`Find gauge pressure ${h} m below the free surface of water. Use ρ=1000 kg/m³ and g=10 m/s².`,value:p,unit:'kPa',decimals:1,step:5,explain:`p=ρgh=1000×10×${h} Pa=${p.toFixed(1)} kPa.`});});
  addFamily('darcy-loss','Fluid Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const f=.02,ratio=20+4*i,V=1+(i%4),g=10,hf=f*ratio*V*V/(2*g);addNum({id,topic:'Fluid Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A pipe has Darcy f=${f}, L/D=${ratio}, mean speed ${V} m/s. Estimate friction head loss using g=10 m/s².`,value:hf,unit:'m',decimals:2,step:.2,explain:`Darcy–Weisbach: h_f=f(L/D)V²/(2g)=${f}×${ratio}×${V}²/20=${hf.toFixed(2)} m.`});});
  addFamily('conduction','Heat Transfer',(i,d,id)=>{const k=round(.5+.1*(i%5),1),A=1+i%4,dt=20+5*i,L=round(.1+.05*(i%4),2),Q=k*A*dt/L;addNum({id,topic:'Heat Transfer',difficulty:d,prompt:`A plane wall has k=${k} W/(m·K), area ${A} m², thickness ${L} m and ΔT=${dt} K. Find steady heat rate.`,value:Q,unit:'W',decimals:0,step:20,explain:`For 1-D steady conduction, Q̇=kAΔT/L=${k}×${A}×${dt}/${L}=${fmt(Q,0)} W.`});});
  addFamily('convection','Heat Transfer',(i,d,id)=>{const h=10+5*i,A=round(.1+.05*(i%4),2),dt=10+2*i,Q=h*A*dt;addNum({id,topic:'Heat Transfer',difficulty:d,prompt:`A surface (area ${A} m²) is at ${dt} K above ambient. If h=${h} W/(m²·K), find convective heat rate.`,value:Q,unit:'W',decimals:1,step:5,explain:`Newton’s cooling law: Q̇=hA(Ts−T∞)=${h}×${A}×${dt}=${Q.toFixed(1)} W.`});});
  addFamily('cutting-speed','Manufacturing',(i,d,id)=>{const D=20+5*(i%8),N=500+100*i,V=Math.PI*D*N/1000;addNum({id,topic:'Manufacturing',difficulty:d,prompt:`A ${D} mm workpiece rotates at ${N} rpm. Estimate cutting speed.`,value:V,unit:'m/min',decimals:1,step:5,explain:`V=πDN/1000=π×${D}×${N}/1000≈${V.toFixed(1)} m/min.`});});
  addFamily('turning-mrr','Manufacturing',(i,d,id)=>{const D=40+5*(i%8),N=200+25*i,f=round(.1+.05*(i%4),2),depth=1+(i%3),mrr=Math.PI*D*N*f*depth/1000;addNum({id,topic:'Manufacturing',difficulty:d,prompt:`In turning, D=${D} mm, N=${N} rpm, feed=${f} mm/rev and depth=${depth} mm. Estimate MRR in cm³/min.`,value:mrr,unit:'cm³/min',decimals:1,step:5,explain:`MRR≈πDNfd/1000=${Math.PI.toFixed(3)}×${D}×${N}×${f}×${depth}/1000≈${mrr.toFixed(1)} cm³/min.`});});
  addFamily('capability','Metrology',(i,d,id)=>{const sigma=round(.25+.25*(i%4),2),target=1+(i%8),width=6*sigma*target;addNum({id,topic:'Metrology',difficulty:d,prompt:`A stable process has specification width ${width.toFixed(2)} mm and σ=${sigma.toFixed(2)} mm. Find Cp.`,value:target,unit:'',decimals:1,step:.5,explain:`Cp=(USL−LSL)/(6σ)=${width.toFixed(2)}/(6×${sigma.toFixed(2)})=${target.toFixed(1)}.`});});
  addFamily('eoq','Industrial Engineering',(i,d,id)=>{const Q=50+10*i,S=50,H=2,D=Q*Q*H/(2*S);addNum({id,topic:'Industrial Engineering',difficulty:d,prompt:`Annual demand D=${D} units, ordering cost S=₹${S}/order and holding cost H=₹${H}/unit-year. Find EOQ.`,value:Q,unit:'units',decimals:0,step:20,explain:`EOQ=√(2DS/H)=√(2×${D}×${S}/${H})=${Q} units under the basic EOQ assumptions.`});});
  addFamily('spring-rate','Machine Design',(i,d,id)=>{const k=10+5*i,x=4+(i%7),F=k*x;addNum({id,topic:'Machine Design',difficulty:d,prompt:`A linear spring extends ${x} mm under a ${F} N load. Find its stiffness.`,value:k,unit:'N/mm',decimals:0,step:5,explain:`For a linear spring, F=kx, so k=F/x=${F}/${x}=${k} N/mm.`});});
  addFamily('motor-power','Cross-core Engineering Basics',(i,d,id)=>{const V=24+4*i,I=2+(i%8),P=V*I;addNum({id,topic:'Cross-core Engineering Basics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A DC motor draws ${I} A from a ${V} V supply. Find electrical input power.`,value:P,unit:'W',decimals:0,step:20,explain:`Electrical input power P=VI=${V}×${I}=${P} W. Shaft output is lower when losses are present.`});});
  addFamily('pump-power','Fluid Mechanics',(i,d,id)=>{const qL=10+2*i,H=10+2*(i%10),eta=.8,P=.01*qL*H/eta;addNum({id,topic:'Fluid Mechanics',difficulty:d,prompt:`A water pump delivers ${qL} L/s through ${H} m head at ${eta*100}% efficiency. Estimate input power (g=10 m/s²).`,value:P,unit:'kW',decimals:2,step:.5,explain:`P_in=ρgQH/η. With Q=${qL}/1000 m³/s, P_in=1000×10×(${qL}/1000)×${H}/${eta}=${P.toFixed(2)} kW.`});});

  // General Aptitude: 16 families × 10 distinct variants = 160 questions.
  const aptitude=(name,fn)=>{for(let i=0;i<10;i++)fn(i,levels[(i+name.length)%3],`AP-${name}-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`);};
  const aptNum=(x)=>addNum({...x,category:'aptitude',role:'General Aptitude'});
  aptitude('discount',(i,d,id)=>{const price=500+100*i,pct=10+5*(i%7),sale=price*(1-pct/100);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Percentages',difficulty:d,prompt:`A tool priced at ₹${price} receives a ${pct}% discount. What is the sale price?`,value:sale,unit:'₹',decimals:0,step:50,explain:`Discount=${pct}% of ₹${price}=₹${price*pct/100}. Sale price=${price}−${price*pct/100}=₹${sale}.`});});
  aptitude('ratio-share',(i,d,id)=>{const a=2+i%5,b=3+(i*2)%6,total=(a+b)*(10+i);const part=total*a/(a+b);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Ratios',difficulty:d,prompt:`₹${total} is divided in the ratio ${a}:${b}. What is the first share?`,value:part,unit:'₹',decimals:0,step:a+b,explain:`Total ratio parts=${a}+${b}=${a+b}. First share=${total}×${a}/${a+b}=₹${part}.`});});
  aptitude('weighted-average',(i,d,id)=>{const n1=10+2*i,n2=8+2*(i%7),m1=60+2*i,m2=70+3*(i%8),avg=(n1*m1+n2*m2)/(n1+n2);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Averages',difficulty:d,prompt:`Group A has ${n1} people averaging ${m1}; group B has ${n2} people averaging ${m2}. Find the combined average.`,value:avg,unit:'',decimals:1,step:2,explain:`Combined average=(n₁m₁+n₂m₂)/(n₁+n₂)=(${n1}×${m1}+${n2}×${m2})/(${n1}+${n2})=${avg.toFixed(1)}.`});});
  aptitude('work-rates',(i,d,id)=>{const a=6+2*i,b=2*a,combined=a*b/(a+b);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Work and Time',difficulty:d,prompt:`Worker A completes a job in ${a} days and B in ${b} days. How many days do they need working together?`,value:combined,unit:'days',decimals:1,step:.5,explain:`Combined rate=1/${a}+1/${b}=${(1/a+1/b).toFixed(3)} job/day. Time=1/rate=${combined.toFixed(1)} days.`});});
  aptitude('distance-speed',(i,d,id)=>{const speed=36+6*i,time=1+(i%5)*.5,dist=speed*time;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Speed and Distance',difficulty:d,prompt:`A vehicle maintains ${speed} km/h for ${time} h. How far does it travel?`,value:dist,unit:'km',decimals:0,step:10,explain:`Distance=speed×time=${speed}×${time}=${dist} km.`});});
  aptitude('simple-interest',(i,d,id)=>{const P=1000+500*i,r=5+(i%6),t=2+(i%4),SI=P*r*t/100;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Interest',difficulty:d,prompt:`Find simple interest on ₹${P} at ${r}% per year for ${t} years.`,value:SI,unit:'₹',decimals:0,step:50,explain:`SI=Prt/100=${P}×${r}×${t}/100=₹${SI}.`});});
  aptitude('compound-interest',(i,d,id)=>{const P=1000+1000*i,r=10,n=2,A=P*Math.pow(1+r/100,n),CI=A-P;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Interest',difficulty:d,prompt:`₹${P} is invested at ${r}% compound interest annually for ${n} years. Find the interest earned.`,value:CI,unit:'₹',decimals:0,step:100,explain:`Amount=P(1+r/100)^n=${P}(1.1)^2=₹${Math.round(A)}. Interest=A−P=₹${Math.round(CI)}.`});});
  aptitude('probability',(i,d,id)=>{const red=2+i,blue=4+2*(i%6),total=red+blue,p=red/total;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Probability',difficulty:d,prompt:`A bag has ${red} red and ${blue} blue counters. One is chosen at random. What is P(red)?`,value:p,unit:'',decimals:3,step:.1,explain:`P(red)=favourable outcomes/total outcomes=${red}/(${red}+${blue})=${p.toFixed(3)}.`});});
  aptitude('permutations',(i,d,id)=>{const n=5+i%5,r=2+i%3;let value=1;for(let k=0;k<r;k++)value*=n-k;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Counting',difficulty:d,prompt:`How many ordered arrangements of ${r} different items can be selected from ${n} distinct items?`,value,unit:'ways',decimals:0,step:10,explain:`Use nPr=n!/(n−r)!=${n}×${Array.from({length:r-1},(_,j)=>n-j-1).join('×')}=${value}.`});});
  aptitude('arithmetic-sequence',(i,d,id)=>{const first=3+2*i,diff=2+i%7,n=8,term=first+(n-1)*diff;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Number Series',difficulty:d,prompt:`An arithmetic sequence starts at ${first} and increases by ${diff} each term. What is its ${n}th term?`,value:term,unit:'',decimals:0,step:diff,explain:`aₙ=a₁+(n−1)d=${first}+(${n}−1)×${diff}=${term}.`});});
  aptitude('profit-percent',(i,d,id)=>{const cp=500+100*i,pct=10+5*(i%8),sp=cp*(1+pct/100);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Profit and Loss',difficulty:d,prompt:`An item costs ₹${cp} and is sold at a ${pct}% profit. Find the selling price.`,value:sp,unit:'₹',decimals:0,step:50,explain:`Selling price=cost×(1+profit rate)=${cp}×${1+pct/100}=₹${sp}.`});});
  aptitude('missing-average',(i,d,id)=>{const count=5+i%6,avg=40+5*i,sum=count*avg,known=sum-(10+3*i);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Averages',difficulty:d,prompt:`The average of ${count} values is ${avg}. The first ${count-1} values sum to ${known}. Find the remaining value.`,value:sum-known,unit:'',decimals:0,step:5,explain:`Total sum=${count}×${avg}=${sum}. Remaining value=${sum}−${known}=${sum-known}.`});});
  aptitude('unit-speed',(i,d,id)=>{const kmh=18*(2+i),ms=kmh/3.6;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Units',difficulty:d,prompt:`Convert ${kmh} km/h to m/s.`,value:ms,unit:'m/s',decimals:0,step:5,explain:`Multiply by 5/18: ${kmh}×5/18=${ms} m/s.`});});
  aptitude('routes',(i,d,id)=>{const a=2+i,b=3+(i%7),routes=a*b;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Counting',difficulty:d,prompt:`There are ${a} routes from A to B and ${b} routes from B to C. How many A-to-C journeys pass through B?`,value:routes,unit:'routes',decimals:0,step:5,explain:`By the multiplication rule, choose one A–B route and one B–C route: ${a}×${b}=${routes}.`});});
  aptitude('successive-percent',(i,d,id)=>{const up=5+5*(i%6),down=5+5*((i+2)%6),factor=round((1+up/100)*(1-down/100),4),net=(factor-1)*100;aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Percentages',difficulty:d,prompt:`A value rises by ${up}% and then falls by ${down}%. Find the net percentage change.`,value:net,unit:'%',decimals:2,step:1,explain:`Multiply the factors: (1+${up}/100)×(1−${down}/100)=${factor}. Net change=(${factor}−1)×100=${net.toFixed(2)}%.`});});
  aptitude('mixture-average',(i,d,id)=>{const m1=2+i%5,m2=3+(2*i)%6,c1=10+5*i,c2=30+4*i,avg=(m1*c1+m2*c2)/(m1+m2);aptNum({id,topic:'Aptitude — Weighted Averages',difficulty:d,prompt:`Mix ${m1} kg of a ₹${c1}/kg material with ${m2} kg of a ₹${c2}/kg material. Find the average price per kg.`,value:avg,unit:'₹/kg',decimals:2,step:2,explain:`Weighted average=(m₁c₁+m₂c₂)/(m₁+m₂)=(${m1}×${c1}+${m2}×${c2})/${m1+m2}=₹${avg.toFixed(2)}/kg.`});});

  if(questions.length!==520)throw new Error(`Expected 520 generated questions, got ${questions.length}`);
  window.MECHPREP_GENERATED_QUESTIONS=questions;
})();

// 5. Expanded Question Bank V2 (expanded-question-bank.js — 1,250 questions)

// Original practice problems built from the topics and methods in the supplied handouts.
// 600 Mechanical + 650 Aptitude/LR/Verbal/Cryptarithmetic variants; no network calls or external dependencies.
(() => {
  const out=[], levels=['Basic','Intermediate','Advanced'];
  const round=(x,d=2)=>Number(x.toFixed(d));
  const fm=(x,d=2)=>{const s=Number(x).toFixed(d);return s.includes('.')?s.replace(/0+$/,'').replace(/\.$/,''):s};
  const sort=(a,id)=>{let h=0;for(const c of id)h=(h*31+c.charCodeAt(0))>>>0;return a.map((v,i)=>({v,k:Math.sin(h+i*29.71)*10000})).sort((x,y)=>x.k-y.k).map(x=>x.v)};
  function add({id,category='mechanical',topic,role,level,prompt,answer,wrong,explain,source,weight='problem'}){
    const opts=sort([String(answer),...wrong.map(String)].filter((x,i,a)=>a.indexOf(x)===i).slice(0,4),id);
    while(opts.length<4){const n=`${answer} (alternate ${opts.length})`;if(!opts.includes(n))opts.push(n)}
    const q={id,category,topic,role, difficulty:level, text:prompt, options:opts, answer:opts.indexOf(String(answer)), explain, source, weightClass:weight, marks:weight==='problem'?4:2};
    if(q.answer<0||opts.length!==4)throw new Error(`Invalid question ${id}`);out.push(q);
  }
  function num({id,topic,role,level,prompt,value,unit='',decimals=2,source,explain,weight='problem',step}){
    const v=round(value,decimals),d=step||Math.max(10**-decimals,Math.abs(v)*.14);
    const wrong=[v+d,v-d,v+2*d,v/2,v*2,v+4*d,v-3*d].map(x=>round(x,decimals)).filter(x=>x!==v);
    const fmtVal=x=>`${fm(x,decimals)}${unit?` ${unit}`:''}`;
    add({id,topic,role,level,prompt,answer:fmtVal(v),wrong:[...new Set(wrong)].slice(0,3).map(fmtVal),source,weight,explain});
  }
  const msrc={
    som:'Strength of Materials 50 Aptitude Questions, supplied PDF pp. 1–4 (stress, deformation, beams, shafts and material behavior)',
    thermo:'Thermodynamics, supplied PDF pp. 1–3 (laws, processes, cycles, engines and refrigeration)',
    fluid:'Fluid_Mechanics, supplied PDF pp. 1–4 (properties, pressure, continuity, Bernoulli, flow and pumps)',
    manuf:'Manufacturing_Process_Aptitude_Test_50_Questions, supplied PDF pp. 1–8 (casting, forming, machining, joining, CNC and metrology)',
    design:'Machine_Design_Aptitude_Test_50_Questions, supplied PDF pp. 1–7 (shafts, gears, springs, bearings, fatigue and components)',
    ie:'Industrial_Engineering_Basics_Aptitude_Test_50_Questions, supplied PDF pp. 1–3 (work study, productivity, inventory, quality and OEE)'
  };
  const aptSrc=(topic,pages)=>`Student Handout V3 (6), ${topic} (printed p. ${pages.student}); QA & LR Trainer Handout V3, ${topic} (printed p. ${pages.qa})`;
  const p=(name,topic,role='Mechanical Core',src,fn)=>{for(let i=0;i<40;i++)fn(i,levels[(i+name.length)%3],`MX-${name}-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`,topic,role,src)};

  p('stress','Strength of Materials','CAE / Analysis',msrc.som,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const P=20+2*i,A=250+25*(i%8),v=P*1000/A;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A uniform tie carries ${P} kN tension over a ${A} mm² area. Find its average normal stress.`,value:v,unit:'MPa',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`Use σ=P/A. Convert ${P} kN to ${P*1000} N: σ=${P*1000}/${A}=${fm(v,1)} N/mm²=${fm(v,1)} MPa.`})});
  p('elongation','Strength of Materials','CAE / Analysis',msrc.som,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const P=10+2*i,L=400+20*(i%10),A=160+20*(i%7),E=200,v=P*L/(A*E*1000);num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A steel rod (${A} mm² area, ${L} mm length) carries ${P} kN axial load. Take E=200 GPa. Estimate its elastic extension.`,value:v,unit:'mm',decimals:3,step:.01,source:s,explain:`δ=PL/(AE). In N/mm², E=200,000. Thus δ=(${P}×1000×${L})/(${A}×200000)=${fm(v,3)} mm.`})});
  p('bending','Strength of Materials','CAE / Analysis',msrc.som,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const b=30+2*(i%8),h=50+2*i,sigma=15+3*i,M=sigma*b*h*h/6;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A rectangular section has b=${b} mm and h=${h} mm. Its second moment is I=bh³/12. If M=${fm(M,0)} N·mm, find the maximum bending stress.`,value:sigma,unit:'MPa',decimals:1,step:4,source:s,explain:`σ=Mc/I. Since c=h/2 and I=bh³/12, σ=6M/(bh²)=6×${fm(M,0)}/(${b}×${h}²)=${fm(sigma,1)} MPa.`})});
  p('torsion','Strength of Materials','CAE / Analysis',msrc.som,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const d=20+2*(i%10),tau=12+2*i,T=tau*Math.PI*d**3/16/1000;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A solid circular shaft of diameter ${d} mm transmits ${fm(T,2)} N·m torque. Find the maximum torsional shear stress.`,value:tau,unit:'MPa',decimals:1,step:3,source:s,explain:`For a solid shaft, τmax=16T/(πd³). Convert T to N·mm: ${fm(T*1000,0)}. Substitution gives τ=${fm(tau,1)} N/mm²=${fm(tau,1)} MPa.`})});
  p('firstlaw','Thermodynamics','Maintenance / Utilities',msrc.thermo,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const Q=200+25*i,W=50+10*(i%12),du=Q-W;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A closed system receives ${Q} kJ heat and does ${W} kJ work. Using work by the system as positive, find ΔU.`,value:du,unit:'kJ',decimals:0,source:s,explain:`First Law for a closed system: ΔU=Q−W=${Q}−${W}=${du} kJ. Check the sign convention before substituting.`})});
  p('thermal-efficiency','Thermodynamics','Maintenance / Utilities',msrc.thermo,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const Qin=500+50*i,eff=20+2*(i%25),Qout=Qin*(1-eff/100);num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A heat engine receives ${Qin} kJ per cycle and rejects ${fm(Qout,0)} kJ. What is its thermal efficiency?`,value:eff,unit:'%',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`η= Wnet/Qin = (Qin−Qout)/Qin×100 = (${Qin}−${fm(Qout,0)})/${Qin}×100=${fm(eff,1)}%.`})});
  p('continuity','Fluid Mechanics','Maintenance / Utilities',msrc.fluid,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const d1=80+4*(i%10),d2=40+2*(i%8),v1=1+.25*i,v2=v1*(d1/d2)**2;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`Steady incompressible water flows from a ${d1} mm pipe section at ${fm(v1,2)} m/s into a ${d2} mm section. Find the velocity in the smaller section.`,value:v2,unit:'m/s',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`Continuity gives A₁V₁=A₂V₂. For circular sections, A∝d², so V₂=V₁(d₁/d₂)²=${fm(v1,2)}×(${d1}/${d2})²=${fm(v2,2)} m/s.`})});
  p('hydrostatic','Fluid Mechanics','Maintenance / Utilities',msrc.fluid,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const rho=1000,g=9.81,h=2+1.5*i,pk=rho*g*h/1000;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`Find the gauge pressure ${h} m below a still water surface. Use ρ=1000 kg/m³ and g=9.81 m/s².`,value:pk,unit:'kPa',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`Hydrostatic pressure p=ρgh=1000×9.81×${h}=${fm(pk,2)} kPa. This is gauge pressure; add atmospheric pressure only when absolute pressure is required.`})});
  p('conduction','Heat Transfer','Maintenance / Utilities','Thermodynamics, supplied PDF pp. 1–3; Heat Transfer chapter in the local MechPrep guide',(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const k=.25+.15*(i%10),A=1+.25*(i%8),dt=20+5*i,L=.05+.01*(i%8),q=k*A*dt/L;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A plane wall has k=${fm(k,2)} W/(m·K), area ${fm(A,2)} m², thickness ${fm(L,2)} m and a ${dt} K temperature difference. Find steady heat rate.`,value:q,unit:'W',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`Fourier conduction through a plane wall: Q̇=kAΔT/L=${fm(k,2)}×${fm(A,2)}×${dt}/${fm(L,2)}=${fm(q,1)} W.`})});
  p('turning-speed','Manufacturing','Manufacturing',msrc.manuf,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const D=20+2*i,N=200+20*(i%15),V=Math.PI*D*N/1000;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`In turning, a ${D} mm workpiece runs at ${N} rpm. Estimate the cutting speed.`,value:V,unit:'m/min',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`V=πDN/1000=${Math.PI.toFixed(3)}×${D}×${N}/1000=${fm(V,1)} m/min (D in mm, N in rpm).`})});
  p('gear-speed','Machine Design','CAD / Design',msrc.design,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const z1=18+2*(i%12),ratio=2+(i%4),z2=z1*ratio,n1=300+25*i,n2=n1/ratio;add({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`An external ${z1}-tooth gear rotating at ${n1} rpm drives a ${z2}-tooth gear. Find the driven speed and direction.`,answer:`${fm(n2,0)} rpm, opposite direction`,wrong:[`${fm(n2,0)} rpm, same direction`,`${fm(n1*ratio,0)} rpm, opposite direction`,`${fm(n1,0)} rpm, same direction`],source:s,explain:`N₁Z₁=N₂Z₂. Thus N₂=${n1}×${z1}/${z2}=${fm(n2,0)} rpm. One external mesh reverses direction.`})});
  p('spring','Machine Design','CAD / Design',msrc.design,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const k=10+2*i,x=5+(i%12),F=k*x;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A linear spring extends ${x} mm under a ${F} N load. Find its stiffness.`,value:k,unit:'N/mm',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`Hooke’s law: F=kx, so k=F/x=${F}/${x}=${fm(k,1)} N/mm.`})});
  p('moment','Engineering Mechanics','Mechanical Core','Original MechPrep Engineering Mechanics study note (moment equilibrium and force-arm relation)',(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const F=100+20*i,arm=.15+.05*(i%6),M=F*arm;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A ${F} N force acts perpendicular to a ${fm(arm,2)} m wrench arm. Find the moment about the pivot.`,value:M,unit:'N·m',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`For a perpendicular force, M=F×r=${F}×${fm(arm,2)}=${fm(M,1)} N·m. If the force is angled, use its perpendicular component.`})});
  p('capability','Metrology','Quality / Metrology',msrc.ie,(i,l,id,t,r,s)=>{const sigma=.1+.05*(Math.floor(i/2)%8),target=.8+.2*(Math.floor(i/2)%8);if(i%2===0){const width=6*sigma*target;num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A stable process has specification width ${fm(width,2)} mm and standard deviation σ=${fm(sigma,2)} mm. Find Cp.`,value:target,unit:'',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`Cp=(USL−LSL)/(6σ)=${fm(width,2)}/(6×${fm(sigma,2)})=${fm(target,2)}. Cp measures spread against tolerance, not centering.`})}else{const USL=10+3*sigma*target,LSL=10-3*sigma*(target+.3),cpk=Math.min((USL-10)/(3*sigma),(10-LSL)/(3*sigma));num({id,topic:t,role:r,level:l,prompt:`A stable process has μ=10.00 mm, σ=${fm(sigma,2)} mm, USL=${fm(USL,2)} mm and LSL=${fm(LSL,2)} mm. Find Cpk.`,value:cpk,unit:'',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`Cpk=min[(USL−μ)/(3σ),(μ−LSL)/(3σ)]=min[${fm((USL-10)/(3*sigma),2)},${fm((10-LSL)/(3*sigma),2)}]=${fm(cpk,2)}.`})}});
  for(let i=0;i<40;i++){
    const local=i<20?i:i-20,v=local%4,k=Math.floor(local/4),id=`MX-process-system-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`;
    if(i<20){
      const src=msrc.manuf,topic='Manufacturing',role='Manufacturing',level=levels[i%3];
      if(k===0){const D=40+10*v,N=250+50*v,speed=Math.PI*D*N/1000;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A ${D} mm workpiece turns at ${N} rpm. Estimate cutting speed in m/min.`,value:speed,unit:'m/min',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`V=πDN/1000=π×${D}×${N}/1000=${fm(speed,1)} m/min.`})}
      else if(k===1){const D=50+10*v,N=300+50*v,f=.15+.05*v,d=1+v%3,mrr=Math.PI*D*N*f*d/1000;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`In turning, D=${D} mm, N=${N} rpm, feed=${fm(f,2)} mm/rev and depth=${d} mm. Estimate MRR in cm³/min.`,value:mrr,unit:'cm³/min',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`MRR≈πDNfd/1000=π×${D}×${N}×${fm(f,2)}×${d}/1000=${fm(mrr,1)} cm³/min.`})}
      else if(k===2){const V1=80+20*v,T1=60+15*v,V2=V1+20,n=.25+.05*v,T2=T1*(V1/V2)**(1/n);num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A tool lasts ${T1} min at ${V1} m/min. With Taylor exponent n=${fm(n,2)}, estimate its life at ${V2} m/min.`,value:T2,unit:'min',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`VTⁿ=C, so T₂=T₁(V₁/V₂)^(1/n)=${T1}×(${V1}/${V2})^(1/${fm(n,2)})=${fm(T2,1)} min.`})}
      else if(k===3){const pitch=.5+.1*v,divs=25+25*v,lc=pitch/divs;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A measuring instrument has a ${fm(pitch,2)} mm pitch and ${divs} divisions. Find the least count.`,value:lc,unit:'mm',decimals:3,source:src,explain:`Least count=pitch/divisions=${fm(pitch,2)}/${divs}=${fm(lc,3)} mm.`,step:.002})}
      else{const h0=20+5*v,h1=15+2*v,red=(h0-h1)/h0*100;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A plate is rolled from ${h0} mm to ${h1} mm thickness. Find percentage thickness reduction.`,value:red,unit:'%',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`Reduction=(h₀−h₁)/h₀×100=(${h0}−${h1})/${h0}×100=${fm(red,1)}%.`})}
    }else{
      const src=msrc.ie,topic='Industrial Engineering',role='Supply Chain / Production',level=levels[i%3];
      if(k===0){const input=20+5*v,output=120+30*v,prod=output/input;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A cell uses ${input} labour-hours to produce ${output} accepted units. Find productivity in units/hour.`,value:prod,unit:'units/hour',decimals:2,source:src,explain:`Productivity=output/input=${output}/${input}=${fm(prod,2)} accepted units per labour-hour.`})}
      else if(k===1){const D=500+250*v,S=40+10*v,H=2+v,Q=Math.sqrt(2*D*S/H);num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`Annual demand D=${D} units, order cost S=₹${S}, and annual holding cost H=₹${H}/unit. Find EOQ.`,value:Q,unit:'units',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`EOQ=√(2DS/H)=√(2×${D}×${S}/${H})=${fm(Q,1)} units under basic EOQ assumptions.`})}
      else if(k===2){const av=24000+6000*v,demand=400+100*v,takt=av/demand;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A line has ${av} seconds available per shift and demand of ${demand} units. Find takt time.`,value:takt,unit:'s/unit',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`Takt=available time/customer demand=${av}/${demand}=${fm(takt,1)} s/unit.`})}
      else if(k===3){const A=.85+.03*v,P=.8+.04*v,Q=.9+.02*v,o=A*P*Q*100;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A machine has availability ${fm(A*100,0)}%, performance ${fm(P*100,0)}% and quality ${fm(Q*100,0)}%. Find OEE.`,value:o,unit:'%',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`OEE=A×P×Q=${fm(A,2)}×${fm(P,2)}×${fm(Q,2)}×100=${fm(o,1)}%. Use decimal fractions in the product.`})}
      else{const sum=180+30*v,stations=3+v,cycle=75+5*v,eff=sum/(stations*cycle)*100;num({id,topic,role,level,prompt:`A production line has ${stations} stations, ${sum} seconds of total task time and a ${cycle} second cycle time. Find line efficiency.`,value:eff,unit:'%',decimals:1,source:src,explain:`Efficiency=total task time/(stations×cycle time)×100=${sum}/(${stations}×${cycle})×100=${fm(eff,1)}%.`})}
    }
  }

  const ap=(name,topic,sourceFn,fn)=>{for(let i=0;i<50;i++)fn(i,levels[(i+name.length)%3],`AX-${name}-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`,topic,sourceFn(i))};
  const qa=(topic,st,qt)=>i=>aptSrc(topic,{student:st,qa:qt});
  ap('number-system','Aptitude — Number System',qa('Number System',5,5),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const a=120+7*i,div=2+(i%8),r=a%div,val=div-r===div?0:div-r;num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`What is the least non-negative number that must be added to ${a} to make it divisible by ${div}?`,value:val,unit:'',decimals:0,source:s,explain:`Divide ${a} by ${div}: remainder=${r}. The next multiple is reached by adding ${div}−${r}=${val}.`,weight:'problem',step:1})});
  ap('percent-profit','Aptitude — Percentages, Profit & Loss',qa('Percentages / Profit, Loss and Discounts',20,27),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const cp=400+40*i,pct=5+5*(i%16),sp=cp*(1+pct/100);num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`A component costs ₹${cp} and is sold at a ${pct}% profit. Find its selling price.`,value:sp,unit:'₹',decimals:0,source:s,explain:`SP=CP×(1+profit%/100)=${cp}×(1+${pct}/100)=₹${fm(sp,0)}. Profit percentage is based on cost price.`,step:20})});
  ap('ratio-average','Aptitude — Ratios & Averages',qa('Ratios, Proportions and Variations / Averages',33,47),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const a=2+i%7,b=3+(2*i)%8,total=(a+b)*(10+i),part=total*a/(a+b);num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`A batch of ${total} components is divided in the ratio ${a}:${b}. How many belong to the first group?`,value:part,unit:'components',decimals:0,source:s,explain:`Total parts=${a}+${b}=${a+b}. First-group count=${total}×${a}/${a+b}=${part}. Check the two shares add to ${total}.`,step:a+b})});
  ap('interest-mixture','Aptitude — Interest & Mixtures',qa('Simple and Compound Interest / Alligations and Mixtures',29,40),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const P=1000+250*i,r=4+2*(i%8),n=2+(i%4),A=P*Math.pow(1+r/100,n),ci=A-P;num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`₹${P} is invested at ${r}% compound interest per year for ${n} years. Find the interest earned.`,value:ci,unit:'₹',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`A=P(1+r/100)^n=${P}(1+${r}/100)^${n}=₹${fm(A,2)}. Compound interest=A−P=₹${fm(ci,2)}.`,step:50})});
  ap('work-rates','Aptitude — Time & Work',qa('Time and Work',51,83),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const a=4+i%13,b=5+(3*i)%14;const days=a*b/(a+b);num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`Worker A completes a job in ${a} days and B in ${b} days. How many days do they need working together at constant rates?`,value:days,unit:'days',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`Rates add: 1/T=1/${a}+1/${b}=(${a+b})/${a*b}. Therefore T=${a*b}/${a+b}=${fm(days,2)} days.`,step:.5})});
  ap('speed-distance','Aptitude — Time, Speed & Distance',qa('Time, Speed and Distance / Trains, Boats and Streams',57,92),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const v=30+5*(i%18),time=1+i%9,dist=v*time;num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`A service vehicle travels at ${v} km/h for ${time} h at constant speed. How far does it travel?`,value:dist,unit:'km',decimals:0,source:s,explain:`Distance=speed×time=${v}×${time}=${dist} km. Keep the time unit consistent with km/h.`,step:10})});
  ap('probability','Aptitude — Probability & Counting',qa('Permutation and Combination / Probability',68,107),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const red=2+i%10,blue=3+2*(i%9),p=red/(red+blue);num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`A box contains ${red} red and ${blue} blue washers. One is selected at random. Find the probability it is red.`,value:p,unit:'',decimals:3,source:s,explain:`For equally likely outcomes, P(red)=favourable/total=${red}/(${red}+${blue})=${fm(p,3)}.`,step:.05})});
  ap('data-interpretation','Aptitude — Data Interpretation',qa('Data Interpretation',81,123),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const a=80+15*i,b=100+10*(i%13),v=a+b;num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`A workshop completes ${a} units in week 1 and ${b} units in week 2. What is its average weekly output?`,value:v/2,unit:'units/week',decimals:1,source:s,explain:`Average=(total output)/(number of weeks)=(${a}+${b})/2=${fm(v/2,1)} units/week.`,step:5})});
  ap('series-coding','Logical Reasoning — Series & Coding',qa('Series / Coding and Decoding',101,151),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const a=3+i, d=2+(i%7),n=8+(i%6),v=a+(n-1)*d;add({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`Find the next term: ${a}, ${a+d}, ${a+2*d}, ${a+3*d}, …`,answer:String(v),wrong:[String(v+d),String(v-1),String(a*n)],source:s,explain:`The sequence is an arithmetic progression with common difference ${d}. Add ${d} to the previous term: next=${a+3*d}+${d}=${v}.`,weight:'short'})});
  ap('direction-relations','Logical Reasoning — Directions & Blood Relations',qa('Direction Sense Test / Blood Relations',98,145),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{const north=2+i%8,east=3+(2*i)%9,dist=Math.sqrt(north*north+east*east);num({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt:`A person walks ${north} km north and then ${east} km east. What is the straight-line distance from the starting point?`,value:dist,unit:'km',decimals:2,source:s,explain:`The path forms a right triangle. Distance=√(${north}²+${east}²)=√${north*north+east*east}=${fm(dist,2)} km.`,step:.5})});
  const nouns=['pumps','valves','gears','bearings','motors','sensors','boilers','compressors','filters','turbines'];
  ap('logic-reasoning','Logical Reasoning — Syllogisms & Arrangement',qa('Syllogisms / Seating Arrangement / Data Sufficiency',114,196),(i,l,id,t,s)=>{
    const a=nouns[Math.floor(i/5)],b=nouns[(Math.floor(i/5)+3)%nouns.length],c=nouns[(Math.floor(i/5)+6)%nouns.length],pattern=i%5;
    const cases=[
      {prompt:`All ${a} are ${b}. Some ${b} are ${c}. Which conclusion must be true?`,answer:`All ${a} are ${b}.`,wrong:[`Some ${a} are ${c}.`,`All ${b} are ${a}.`,`No ${a} are ${c}.`],explain:`The first sentence is a premise and therefore guaranteed. The second does not show that any of the ${a} are in the ${b} subset that is ${c}.`},
      {prompt:`No ${a} are ${b}. All ${c} are ${a}. Which conclusion follows?`,answer:`No ${c} are ${b}.`,wrong:[`All ${b} are ${c}.`,`Some ${c} are ${b}.`,`No ${a} are ${c}.`],explain:`Every ${c} belongs to ${a}, and no ${a} belongs to ${b}; therefore no ${c} can be ${b}.`},
      {prompt:`Some ${a} are ${b}. All ${b} are ${c}. What follows?`,answer:`Some ${a} are ${c}.`,wrong:[`All ${a} are ${c}.`,`No ${a} are ${c}.`,`All ${c} are ${b}.`],explain:`The particular ${a} that are ${b} must also be ${c}; the premise does not say every ${a} is ${b}.`},
      {prompt:`All ${a} are ${b}. No ${b} are ${c}. Which conclusion is certain?`,answer:`No ${a} are ${c}.`,wrong:[`Some ${a} are ${c}.`,`All ${c} are ${a}.`,`No ${a} are ${b}.`],explain:`If every ${a} is in the set ${b} and the sets ${b} and ${c} are disjoint, ${a} and ${c} are disjoint.`},
      {prompt:`Some ${a} are ${b}, and some ${b} are ${c}. What can be concluded about ${a} and ${c}?`,answer:`No definite relation follows.`,wrong:[`Some ${a} are ${c}.`,`All ${a} are ${c}.`,`No ${a} are ${c}.`],explain:`The two “some ${b}” groups may be different members. They do not prove overlap between ${a} and ${c}.`}
    ][pattern];
    add({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,...cases,source:s,weight:'short'});
  });
  ap('cryptarithm','Logical Reasoning — Cryptarithmetic',i=>`Student Handout V3 (6), Cryptarithmetic (printed p. 155); QA & LR Trainer Handout V3 (printed p. 224)`,(i,l,id,t,s)=>{
    const letters='ABCDEFGHIJ',offset=(i*2)%7,pat=i%3,A=letters[offset],B=letters[offset+1],C=letters[offset+2],D=letters[offset+3],E=letters[offset+4],F=letters[offset+5],G=letters[offset+6];
    let count=0,prompt='',explain='';
    if(pat===0){prompt=`In ${A}+${B}=${C}${D}, each letter is a distinct digit and ${C} is nonzero. How many assignments satisfy the addition?`;for(let a=0;a<=9;a++)for(let b=0;b<=9;b++)if(a!==b){const n=a+b,c=Math.floor(n/10),d=n%10;if(c>0&&new Set([a,b,c,d]).size===4)count++}explain=`Check the units column first: ${A}+${B} gives units ${D} and carry ${C}. Enumerate unordered digit pairs, reject repeated digits and a leading zero; ${count} assignments remain.`}
    else if(pat===1){prompt=`For ${A}${B}+${C}=${D}${E}, all five letters are different digits and ${A}, ${D} are nonzero. How many solutions are there?`;for(let a=1;a<=9;a++)for(let b=0;b<=9;b++)for(let c=0;c<=9;c++){const n=10*a+b+c,d=Math.floor(n/10),e=n%10;if(d<10&&new Set([a,b,c,d,e]).size===5)count++}explain=`For each distinct choice of ${A}, ${B} and ${C}, calculate ${A}${B}+${C}; keep it only when the two result digits are distinct from each other and all input digits, with a nonzero leading digit. Count=${count}.`}
    else{prompt=`For ${A}${B}+${C}${D}=${E}${F}${G}, the six addend letters are distinct digits and ${A}, ${C}, ${E} are nonzero. How many assignments satisfy it?`;for(let a=1;a<=9;a++)for(let b=0;b<=9;b++)for(let c=1;c<=9;c++)for(let d=0;d<=9;d++){if(new Set([a,b,c,d]).size<4)continue;const n=10*a+b+10*c+d,e=Math.floor(n/100),f=Math.floor(n/10)%10,g=n%10;if(e>0&&new Set([a,b,c,d,e,f,g]).size===7)count++}explain=`Work column by column or enumerate the four addend digits, compute their sum, then reject any repeated digit and any leading zero. The exact distinct-letter count is ${count}.`}
    add({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,prompt,answer:String(count),wrong:[String(count+1),String(Math.max(0,count-1)),String(count+3)],source:s,explain,weight:'short'});
  });
  const subjects=['pump','valve','bearing','sensor','gear','boiler','compressor','actuator','filter','turbine'];
  ap('verbal-reasoning','Verbal Ability — Grammar & Critical Reasoning',i=>`Student Handout V3 (6), Verbal Ability (printed pp. 158–220)`,(i,l,id,t,s)=>{
    const base=subjects[Math.floor(i/5)],plural=base+'s',pattern=i%5;
    const cases=[
      {prompt:'Choose the grammatically correct sentence:',answer:`The ${base} has been inspected before startup.`,wrong:[`The ${base} have been inspected before startup.`,`The ${base} is been inspected before startup.`,`The ${base} has inspect before startup.`],explain:`“${base}” is singular, so it takes “has”; the passive present-perfect form is “has been inspected.”`},
      {prompt:'Choose the grammatically correct sentence:',answer:`The ${plural} have passed the pressure check.`,wrong:[`The ${plural} has passed the pressure check.`,`The ${plural} have pass the pressure check.`,`The ${plural} is passed the pressure check.`],explain:`The plural subject “${plural}” takes “have”; the past participle is “passed.”`},
      {prompt:'Choose the sentence with correct tense and agreement:',answer:`The inspector recorded the ${plural} after each test.`,wrong:[`The inspector record the ${plural} after each test.`,`The inspectors records the ${plural} after each test.`,`The inspector has recorded the ${plural} yesterday.`],explain:`A singular inspector in simple past uses “recorded.” The sentence gives a completed repeated action.`},
      {prompt:'Choose the grammatically correct sentence:',answer:`Each of the ${plural} is checked before release.`,wrong:[`Each of the ${plural} are checked before release.`,`Each of the ${plural} have checked before release.`,`Each of the ${plural} were checks before release.`],explain:`The head word “Each” is singular, so the verb is “is,” even though “${plural}” is plural.`},
      {prompt:'Choose the grammatically correct sentence:',answer:`Neither the operator nor the ${plural} were available for the inspection.`,wrong:[`Neither the operator nor the ${plural} was available for the inspection.`,`Neither the operator or the ${plural} were available for the inspection.`,`Neither the operator nor the ${plural} is available yesterday.`],explain:`With “neither…nor,” the verb conventionally agrees with the nearer subject, here plural “${plural}”; “nor” pairs with “neither.”`}
    ][pattern];
    add({id,category:'aptitude',topic:t,role:'General Aptitude',level:l,...cases,source:s,weight:'short'});
  });

  if(out.length!==1250)throw new Error(`Expected 1250 questions, received ${out.length}`);
  window.MECHPREP_GENERATED_QUESTIONS_V2=out;
})();

// 6. Train & Relative Speed Practice Bank (train-practice.js — 20 questions)

// Original aptitude practice based on the supplied trains/relative-speed topics.
(() => {
  const q=[],source='Student Handout V3 (6) · Trains, Boats and Streams, Races (printed p. 62)';
  const add=(text,options,answer,explain,level='Basic')=>q.push({id:`TRAIN-${String(q.length+1).padStart(2,'0')}`,category:'aptitude',topic:'Trains and Relative Speed',role:'General Aptitude',difficulty:level,text,options,answer,explain,source,weightClass:'problem',marks:4,trick:'Convert every speed to m/s when lengths are in metres. Use the full distance the train must clear, then divide by relative speed.'});
  add('A train travels at 60 km/h and passes a pole in 9 seconds. What is the train length?',['120 m','180 m','324 m','150 m'],3,'60×5/18=16.67 m/s. Passing a pole means distance equals train length: L=16.67×9=150 m.');
  add('A 125 m train passes a man walking at 5 km/h in the same direction in 10 seconds. Find the train speed.',['45 km/h','50 km/h','54 km/h','55 km/h'],1,'Relative speed=125/10=12.5 m/s=45 km/h. Same direction: train speed=relative speed+man speed=45+5=50 km/h.','Intermediate');
  add('A 180 m train at 54 km/h passes a 120 m platform. How long does it take?',['12 s','18 s','20 s','24 s'],2,'Total distance=180+120=300 m. Speed=54×5/18=15 m/s. Time=300/15=20 s.');
  add('A 150 m train at 72 km/h passes through a 250 m tunnel. Find the time from entry to clearance.',['15 s','20 s','25 s','30 s'],1,'Distance cleared=150+250=400 m. Speed=72×5/18=20 m/s. Time=400/20=20 s.');
  add('Two trains of lengths 120 m and 180 m travel toward each other at 36 km/h and 54 km/h. How long until they completely cross?',['8 s','10 s','12 s','15 s'],2,'Distance=120+180=300 m. Opposite-direction relative speed=(36+54)×5/18=25 m/s. Time=300/25=12 s.','Intermediate');
  add('Two trains of lengths 125 m and 100 m move in the same direction at 72 km/h and 54 km/h. How long for the faster train to pass the slower one?',['35 s','40 s','45 s','50 s'],2,'Distance=125+100=225 m. Relative speed=(72−54)×5/18=5 m/s. Time=225/5=45 s.','Intermediate');
  add('A 100 m train passes a pole in 8 seconds. What is its speed?',['36 km/h','40 km/h','45 km/h','54 km/h'],2,'Speed=100/8=12.5 m/s. Convert to km/h: 12.5×18/5=45 km/h.');
  add('A train crosses a 144 m bridge in 18 seconds. If the train is 216 m long, find its speed.',['60 km/h','68 km/h','72 km/h','80 km/h'],2,'Distance=216+144=360 m. Speed=360/18=20 m/s=72 km/h.');
  add('Two trains of total length 250 m travel in opposite directions at 60 km/h and 40 km/h. How long do they take to cross?',['7.5 s','9 s','12 s','15 s'],1,'Relative speed=100 km/h=27.78 m/s. Time=250/27.78≈9 s.','Intermediate');
  add('Two trains of lengths 240 m and 160 m move in the same direction at 54 km/h and 36 km/h. How long for the faster train to pass?',['60 s','72 s','80 s','90 s'],2,'Combined length=400 m; relative speed=18 km/h=5 m/s. Time=400/5=80 s.','Intermediate');
  add('A 72 km/h train passes a pole in 10 seconds. What is its length?',['180 m','200 m','220 m','250 m'],1,'72 km/h=20 m/s. Train length=distance=speed×time=20×10=200 m.');
  add('A 150 m train moving at 54 km/h crosses a platform in 20 seconds. What is the platform length?',['100 m','125 m','150 m','180 m'],2,'54 km/h=15 m/s; total distance=15×20=300 m. Platform=300−150=150 m.');
  add('A 100 m train at 72 km/h passes a 50 m platform. What is the crossing time?',['5 s','7.5 s','9 s','12 s'],1,'Distance=100+50=150 m. Speed=20 m/s. Time=150/20=7.5 s.');
  add('A train 225 m long passes a pole in 15 seconds. What is its speed in km/h?',['45 km/h','50 km/h','54 km/h','60 km/h'],2,'Speed=225/15=15 m/s=15×18/5=54 km/h.');
  add('A 200 m train crosses a 300 m tunnel at 25 m/s. How long from entering to completely leaving?',['12 s','16 s','20 s','24 s'],2,'Total distance=200+300=500 m; time=500/25=20 s.');
  add('A train passes a stationary signal in 12 seconds at 15 m/s. What distance does the train clear?',['120 m','150 m','180 m','200 m'],2,'For a point object, the train clears its own length: L=vt=15×12=180 m.');
  add('A 180 m train overtakes a cyclist moving at 9 km/h in the same direction in 12 seconds. What is the train speed?',['54 km/h','60 km/h','63 km/h','66 km/h'],2,'Relative speed=180/12=15 m/s=54 km/h. Add cyclist speed: 54+9=63 km/h.','Intermediate');
  add('A 120 m train moving at 45 km/h passes a person walking toward it at 9 km/h. How long does the train take to pass?',['6 s','8 s','10 s','12 s'],1,'Opposite-direction relative speed=(45+9)×5/18=15 m/s. Time=120/15=8 s.');
  add('A 300 m train travelling at 90 km/h passes a pole. How many seconds does it take?',['10 s','12 s','15 s','18 s'],1,'90 km/h=25 m/s. Time=300/25=12 s.');
  add('When two trains move in opposite directions, which relative-speed rule applies?',['Subtract their speeds','Add their speeds','Use only the faster speed','Average their speeds'],1,'Opposite directions increase separation/closing speed, so relative speed is the sum.','Basic');
  window.MECHPREP_TRAIN_QUESTIONS=q;
})();

