  add('Theory of Machines · Mobility','A planar four-link chain has four revolute lower pairs. What is its generic mobility?', ['0','1','2','3'],1,'M=3(L−1)−2J₁=3(3)−2(4)=1.',''+K+' · Unit I','short');
  add('Theory of Machines · Grashof','Four links measure 20, 40, 50 and 70 mm. Which statement follows from Grashof’s criterion?', ['No link can rotate fully','S+L=P+Q, a change-point boundary','It is non-Grashof','The mechanism has zero mobility'],1,'Shortest+longest=20+70=90 and the other pair=40+50=90, so equality is the change-point boundary.',K+' · Unit I','short');
  add('Theory of Machines · Quick return','A crank sweeps 210° on the cutting stroke and 150° on return at constant speed. What is cutting/return time ratio?', ['0.714','1.00','1.40','2.40'],2,'Time is proportional to crank angle at constant angular speed: 210/150=1.40.',K+' · Unit I');
  add('Theory of Machines · Relative motion','A point is 0.15 m from a fixed pivot rotating at 8 rad/s. Its normal acceleration is:', ['1.2 m/s²','9.6 m/s²','64 m/s²','120 m/s²'],1,'a_n=ω²r=8²×0.15=9.6 m/s², directed toward the pivot.',K+' · Unit II');
  add('Theory of Machines · Cam motion','For SHM follower rise, what displacement occurs halfway through the rise angle?', ['0','h/4','h/2','h'],2,'y=(h/2)[1−cos(πθ/β)]; with θ=β/2, cos(π/2)=0, hence y=h/2.',K+' · Unit III','short');
  add('Theory of Machines · Gear trains','A 24-tooth driver at 500 rpm drives a 60-tooth external gear. Find output speed and direction.', ['200 rpm, opposite','200 rpm, same','1,250 rpm, opposite','1,250 rpm, same'],0,'N₂=N₁T₁/T₂=500×24/60=200 rpm. One external mesh reverses direction.',K+' · Unit IV');
  add('Theory of Machines · Friction','If coefficient of friction μ=0.25, the friction angle is approximately:', ['7.1°','14.0°','25.0°','75.0°'],1,'tan φ=μ, so φ=tan⁻¹(0.25)≈14.0°.',K+' · Unit V','short');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Properties','A fluid has dynamic viscosity 0.004 Pa·s and density 800 kg/m³. Its kinematic viscosity is:', ['3.2×10⁻⁶ m²/s','5×10⁻⁶ m²/s','3.2×10⁻³ m²/s','5 m²/s'],1,'ν=μ/ρ=0.004/800=5×10⁻⁶ m²/s.',F+' · Chapter I');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Pressure','A water manometer column difference is 0.45 m. The pressure difference is approximately:', ['0.44 kPa','4.41 kPa','44.1 kPa','441 kPa'],1,'Δp=ρgΔh=1000×9.81×0.45=4414.5 Pa≈4.41 kPa.',F+' · Chapter II');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Hydrostatics','A vertical plane area of 0.8 m² has centroid 2 m below a water surface. Resultant hydrostatic force is:', ['1.57 kN','7.85 kN','15.7 kN','78.5 kN'],2,'F=ρg h̄A=1000×9.81×2×0.8=15,696 N≈15.7 kN.',F+' · Chapter III');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Continuity','Steady water flows at 3 m/s through area 0.06 m², then enters area 0.02 m². Downstream speed is:', ['1 m/s','3 m/s','6 m/s','9 m/s'],3,'A₁V₁=A₂V₂, so V₂=0.06×3/0.02=9 m/s.',F+' · Chapter IV');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Reynolds number','Water (ρ=1000 kg/m³, μ=0.001 Pa·s) flows at 2 m/s in a 25 mm pipe. Reynolds number is:', ['5,000','25,000','50,000','500,000'],2,'Re=ρVD/μ=1000×2×0.025/0.001=50,000.',F+' · Chapter V');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Pipe losses','Using Darcy friction factor f=0.02, L=50 m, D=0.1 m and V=2 m/s, Darcy head loss is about:', ['0.20 m','2.04 m','20.4 m','40.8 m'],1,'h_f=f(L/D)V²/(2g)=0.02×500×4/19.62≈2.04 m.',F+' · Chapter V');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Energy equation','At equal elevations with negligible losses, water speed rises from 2 to 6 m/s. The pressure change p₂−p₁ is:', ['+16 kPa','−16 kPa','+32 kPa','−32 kPa'],1,'Bernoulli gives p₂−p₁=½ρ(V₁²−V₂²)=500(4−36)=−16,000 Pa.',F+' · Chapter IV');
  add('Foundry Lab · Sand permeability','What is the main purpose of a permeability test on moulding sand?', ['Measure cutting speed','Assess gas escape through the sand','Measure metal hardness','Find the pattern draft angle'],1,'Permeability indicates how readily gases pass through the mould; poor venting can cause gas defects.',W+' · Unit 1');
  add('Foundry Lab · Sand testing','Why are sand strength and moisture checked during foundry practice?', ['To choose lathe rpm','To ensure mould holds shape and process condition is controlled','To determine gear ratio','To calculate pump head'],1,'Sand properties influence mould integrity and gas flow; controlled tests help keep casting conditions repeatable.',W+' · Unit 1','short');
  add('Foundry Lab · Forging','In upsetting a cylindrical billet, the axial length decreases. Under ideal volume constancy, its cross-sectional area:', ['decreases','remains fixed','increases','becomes zero'],2,'A₁L₁≈A₂L₂. If length falls, area must rise to conserve volume.',W+' · Unit 2','short');
  add('Foundry Lab · Forging','Which operation primarily lengthens a bar while reducing its cross-section?', ['Upsetting','Drawing out','Punching','Blanking'],1,'Drawing out elongates the workpiece by compressive blows while reducing its section.',W+' · Unit 2','short');
  add('CAMD · Exam pattern','The supplied CAMD paper allocates 15, 15, 15, 15 and 40 marks. What is the total?', ['75','85','95','100'],3,'The visible paper pattern totals 15+15+15+15+40=100 marks.',C+' · question paper');
  add('CAMD · Views','Which view best reveals an internal feature hidden in an object?', ['Auxiliary view only','Sectional view','Isometric view only','Title block'],1,'A sectional view exposes interior features using a cutting plane and section conventions.',C+' · question paper','short');
  add('CAMD · Assembly','For an assembly drawing, what should be established before adding detailed dimensions?', ['Principal axes and component alignment','Hatching density','Border color','Sheet number order'],0,'Establish the assembly relationship and principal axes first, then project views and add dimensions.',C+' · question paper','short');
  add('Applied Mathematics · Taylor','For ln(x) about x=1, let h=x−1. The fourth-degree polynomial is:', ['h+h²/2+h³/3+h⁴/4','h−h²/2+h³/3−h⁴/4','1+h+h²/2+h³/6','h−h²+h³−h⁴'],1,'The series ln(1+h)=h−h²/2+h³/3−h⁴/4+… for |h|<1.',D+' · Unit 3');
  add('Applied Mathematics · Taylor','Using ln(1+h)≈h−h²/2, estimate ln(1.1).', ['0.0495','0.0950','0.1000','0.1050'],1,'h=0.1 gives 0.1−0.01/2=0.095.',D+' · Unit 3');
  add('Mechanics of Materials · Modulus','A normal stress of 120 MPa causes elastic strain 0.0006. Young’s modulus is:', ['20 GPa','72 GPa','200 GPa','720 GPa'],2,'E=σ/ε=120 MPa/0.0006=200,000 MPa=200 GPa.',M+' · scanned question bank concepts');
  add('Mechanics of Materials · Shear modulus','Shear stress is 40 MPa and shear strain is 0.0005. The shear modulus is:', ['20 MPa','20 GPa','80 GPa','200 GPa'],2,'G=τ/γ=40/0.0005=80,000 MPa=80 GPa.',M+' · scanned question bank concepts');
  add('Basic Thermodynamics · First law','A closed system receives 800 kJ heat and does 300 kJ work. With work by system positive, ΔU is:', ['−1100 kJ','−500 kJ','500 kJ','1100 kJ'],2,'ΔU=Q−W=800−300=500 kJ.',B+' · visible module 1–2 concepts');
  add('Basic Thermodynamics · Temperature','A Carnot engine operates between 600 K and 300 K. Its maximum efficiency is:', ['25%','40%','50%','100%'],2,'η=1−T_L/T_H=1−300/600=0.50.',B+' · thermodynamic fundamentals','short');
  add('Basic Thermodynamics · Specific heat','For an ideal gas, which relation is correct?', ['c_p−c_v=R','c_p+c_v=R','c_p/c_v=R','c_v−c_p=R'],0,'For an ideal gas, Mayer’s relation is c_p−c_v=R.',B+' · thermodynamic fundamentals','short');
  add('Applied Mathematics · Taylor','For f(x)=ln x expanded at x=1, what is the linear approximation to ln(0.9)?', ['−0.1','−0.005','0.1','0.105'],0,'First degree: ln x≈(x−1); at x=0.9 this gives −0.1.',D+' · Unit 3');
  add('Theory of Machines · Mobility','For a planar chain with 6 links, 7 lower pairs and no higher pairs, generic mobility is:', ['1','2','3','4'],0,'M=3(6−1)−2(7)=15−14=1.',K+' · Unit I');
  add('Theory of Machines · Gear train','A 30-tooth driver runs at 900 rpm and drives a 90-tooth external gear. Output is:', ['300 rpm opposite','300 rpm same','2700 rpm opposite','2700 rpm same'],0,'N₂=N₁T₁/T₂=900×30/90=300 rpm; external mesh reverses direction.',K+' · Unit IV');
  add('Fluid Mechanics · Dynamic viscosity','A plate of area 0.5 m² moves at 0.4 m/s over a 2 mm oil film, μ=0.08 Pa·s. Viscous force is:', ['4 N','8 N','16 N','32 N'],1,'For linear Couette flow, F=μAU/y=0.08×0.5×0.4/0.002=8 N.',F+' · Chapter I');
